WN.lb(2,{"mt_6lHBTwQPrS":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which question word asks about a place?","choices":["Where","When","Why"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where and when sound alike. Which one is about a place?","explain":"Where asks about a place: Where is the dog? Under the tree."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The answer is \"By bus.\" Which word started the question?","choices":["How","Who","When"],"answer":0,"hint":"By bus tells you the way something happens.","explain":"By bus is the way, so the question was How: How did you get to school?"},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The answer is \"Grandma.\" Which question fits?","choices":["Who is coming to tea?","What is for tea?","When is tea?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Grandma is a person. Which word asks about a person?","explain":"Grandma is a person, so the question starts with Who."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which sentence is a question?","choices":["Who ate the cake?","When it rains, I wear boots.","Tom ate the cake."],"answer":0,"hint":"A question asks something. Starting with when is not enough.","explain":"Who ate the cake? asks something. When it rains, I wear boots starts with when but tells you something."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Max has {n} stickers. Mum asks, \"What is on your stickers?\" Which answer fits?","choices":["Dinosaurs.","In my bag.","Yesterday."],"answer":0,"hint":"What asks about a thing. Which answer is a thing?","explain":"What wants a thing, so dinosaurs fits. In my bag answers Where and yesterday answers When.","params":{"n":[4,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Kim is {n}. She wants to know how old Jo is and writes \"How old are you\". What goes at the end?","choices":["a question mark","a full stop","nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it ask, or does it tell?","explain":"Kim is {n} and is asking Jo something, so \"How old are you?\" needs a question mark.","params":{"n":[5,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"When is your party?\" Which is the best answer?","choices":["On Saturday.","At my house.","Because I am six."],"answer":0,"hint":"When wants a time. Which answer is a time?","explain":"When asks about a time, so on Saturday fits. At my house answers Where; because I am six answers Why."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which word often starts the answer to a why question?","choices":["because","under","at"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why asks for a reason. Which word gives a reason?","explain":"A why question wants a reason, and reasons often start with because."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which question word asks about a person?","choices":["Who","Where","When"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the park picture, which word lit up Mia and Sam?","explain":"Who asks about a person: Who is kicking the ball? Mia is."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"The answer is \"At {n} o'clock.\" Which word started the question?","choices":["When","Where","Who"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is {n} o'clock a person, a place or a time?","explain":"{n} o'clock is a time, so the question started with When.","params":{"n":[1,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Why is Leo crying?\" Which is the best answer?","choices":["Because he fell over.","In the playground.","At playtime."],"answer":0,"hint":"Why wants a reason. Which answer starts with because?","explain":"Why asks for a reason: because he fell over. In the playground answers Where, and at playtime answers When."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ella has {n} toy cars. She asks Dad about the red one. Which question ends with a question mark?","choices":["Where is my red car?","My red car is under the bed","I like my red car"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one asks something and wants an answer?","explain":"Where is my red car? asks something, so it ends with a question mark. The other two tell, so they would end with a full stop.","params":{"n":[3,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words start a question. Who, what, where, when, why and how.","Each word wants a different answer. Who wants a person. What wants a thing. Where wants a place.","When wants a time. Why wants a reason. You often say because. How wants the way. By bus! On my bike!","A question asks. It ends with a question mark. It looks like a hook: ?"],"adult":["The six wh-question words (who, what, where, when, why, how) each signal the type of information wanted: a person, a thing or action, a place, a time, a reason or a manner. Children who can match the word to the kind of answer understand stories and instructions better and can ask for exactly what they want to know. The US Common Core standard L.K.1d asks five-year-olds to understand and use these words, and the English national curriculum adds the question mark in Year 1.","Who, what and where are usually understood first, and when, why and how later, because they deal with time, cause and manner, which are less concrete than people and places. Keep it concrete: ask about a picture, a book or what is happening now.","Common mistakes: mixing up where and when, which sound alike, so a child answers a when question with a place; answering a why question with what happened instead of a reason; thinking any sentence that starts with a question word is a question, as in When it rains, I wear boots; and leaving off the question mark or using a full stop.","At home, play a picture game. You ask one question with each word about a page of a book, and your child points or answers. Then swap: your child asks you, and you check together which word fits the answer you gave. Listen for because after why."]},"familyTip":"Pick a picture in a book and take turns asking one who, what, where, when, why and how question about it. If an answer doesn't fit, ask \"Does that answer the word we used?\"","keyIdea":"Question words tell you what kind of answer is wanted: who asks about a person, what about a thing, where about a place, when about a time, why for a reason (often with because) and how about the way something happens. A question ends with a question mark.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does this answer tell you a place or a time? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A place: answers Where","A time: answers When"],"items":[["In the garden",0],["Under the bed",0],["At the park",0],["At bedtime",1],["On Monday",1],["After lunch",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagqw","caption":"Flagship lab: see what each question word asks about, answer questions about a park picture, pick the word for an answer, and end sentences with ? or .","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9yFAtUkoYr":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which sentence is clear?","choices":["Dan dropped his cup, and the cup broke.","Dan put the cup on the plate, and it broke.","He dropped it, and it broke."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a pronoun that could mean two things, or one with no name to point to.","explain":"Saying the cup again makes it clear. In the second, it could be the cup or the plate. In the third, we never learn who he is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"My {n} friends and I made the cake ___.","choices":["ourselves","ourself","us"],"answer":0,"hint":"My friends and I means we. What is the -self word for we?","explain":"We did it, so use ourselves. That is true for all {n} friends and you together.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"\"The cat chased the {n} mice until ___ hid.\" Which is clearest?","choices":["the mice","it","he"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who hid? A pronoun could point to the cat or the mice.","explain":"Saying the mice makes it clear that all {n} mice hid. It or he could be the cat.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which pronoun fits? \"The tree lost ___ leaves.\"","choices":["its","their","her"],"answer":0,"hint":"A tree is one thing, not a person.","explain":"One thing that is not a person takes its: the tree lost its leaves. No apostrophe here."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A child changes every Sam in a story to he, even the first one. What goes wrong?","choices":["The reader never finds out who he is","Nothing, it is shorter","The story gets too long"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pronoun has to point back to a name.","explain":"A pronoun points back to a name. If Sam is never named, the reader cannot know who he is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"\"Nia saw ___ in the mirror.\" Nia is looking at her own face. Which word?","choices":["herself","her","sheself"],"answer":0,"hint":"Nia sees her own self. Her alone would mean another girl.","explain":"Herself loops back to Nia. Her would mean she saw some other girl, and sheself is not a word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"\"Mum told Gran she was tired.\" It was Mum who was tired. Which is the clearest fix?","choices":["Mum said to Gran, \"I am tired.\"","She told her she was tired.","Mum told Gran she was very tired."],"answer":0,"hint":"Make it plain who was tired. Could anyone still mix them up?","explain":"\"I am tired\" can only mean Mum, the speaker. More pronouns, or adding very, leave it muddled."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the best way to write \"Kim rode Kim's bike\"?","choices":["Kim rode her bike.","She rode her bike.","Kim rode Kim's bike."],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the name once, then use a pronoun for the repeat.","explain":"Kim rode her bike names Kim once and then her points back. She rode her bike never says who she is."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"The {n} ducks shook ___ wings. Which word fits?","choices":["their","its","his"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it one duck or many ducks?","explain":"There are {n} ducks, more than one, so the pronoun is their: they shook their wings.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Lucy told Meg she had won.\" What is the problem?","choices":["She could be Lucy or Meg","There is no problem","It should say he"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: who had won? Could it be two people?","explain":"She could point to Lucy or to Meg, so we cannot tell who won. A name would fix it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Jay painted all {n} pictures ___.","choices":["himself","hisself","him"],"answer":0,"hint":"Jay did it to or for his own self. Which -self word is standard?","explain":"Jay painted all {n} pictures himself. Hisself is not standard, and him would mean someone else.","params":{"n":[3,10]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Sam packed Sam's bag. That sounds odd! We swap the second Sam for a short word: Sam packed his bag. Words like he, his, she, her, it and they are pronouns.","Say the name first. Then the pronoun points back to it. The pronoun must fit. Mia lost her hat. The dogs wagged their tails. The box fell, and it broke.","Watch out for muddles. Tom told Jack he was late. Who was late? We can't tell! Use a name to fix it: Tom told Jack, \"I am late.\"","When you do something to you, add self. I made it myself. Ben hurt himself. They helped themselves. Hisself and theirselves are not right."],"adult":["At seven to nine, children learn to use pronouns to avoid repetition and to link sentences together. They also learn to spot when a pronoun is unclear. This is part of the Year 3 and 4 grammar programme. It covers personal pronouns (he, she, it, they), possessives (his, her, their, our) and reflexive pronouns (myself, himself, themselves).","The lab gives each skill its own action. Children tap repeated names to swap them, but must keep the first mention. They fill gaps while an arrow points back to the noun. They pick a clear rewrite when he could mean two people. They choose -self words that loop back to the doer. Then they find the muddled pronoun in short passages.","Common mistakes: replacing every name, including the first, so the reader never learns who he is; mismatching number, as in the dogs wagged its tails; leaving two possible people for one pronoun; and the non-standard forms hisself and theirselves. Another slip is using him or her where a -self word is needed, which changes the meaning.","To help, read your child's writing aloud and pause at each he, she or it. Ask: who is that? If there are two answers, add the name."]},"familyTip":"When your child tells a story about two friends, stop at a he or she and ask, \"Which one?\" Then help them put the name back in so it is clear.","keyIdea":"Pronouns such as he, she, it and they stand in for a noun so you do not repeat it. Say the name first, then the pronoun points back to it, and it must match: one or many, a person or a thing. If a pronoun could mean two people, use a name or reword it. When someone does something to their own self, use a -self word such as myself or themselves.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each sentence clear, or could the pronoun mean two things? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Clear","❓ Muddled"],"items":[["Nell fed her cat.",0],["The birds built their nest.",0],["I washed myself.",0],["The cup fell, and it broke.",0],["Ali saw Ben, and he ran.",1],["Mum told Gran she was tired.",1],["The boys met the men, and they won.",1],["Amy and Zoe left. She cried.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpron","caption":"Flagship lab: swap repeated names for pronouns, match pronouns to nouns, fix sentences where he could mean two people, and pick -self words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_yBJyCfhtem":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is a verb?","choices":["eat","cake","kitchen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one sounds right after I can?","explain":"Eat is a verb: I can eat. Cake names a thing and kitchen names a place, so they are nouns."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one is a sentence?","choices":["The bird sings.","The sings bird.","Sings the bird."],"answer":0,"hint":"First say who it is, then what it does.","explain":"The bird sings. The naming word bird comes first, then the doing word sings."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Is sleep a verb?","choices":["Yes, it is something you do","No, you keep still","No, it names a thing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does I can sleep sound right?","explain":"Sleep is a verb. It is something you do, even though you keep still."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which word names a person?","choices":["teacher","run","beach"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a word for someone, not a place or an action.","explain":"Teacher names a person, so it is a noun. Beach is a noun for a place, and run is a verb."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia reads {n} books. Which word is the noun that names a thing?","choices":["books","reads","Mia"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mia is a person. Which word names a thing?","explain":"Books names a thing, so it is a noun. Mia read {n} of them, and reads is the verb.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The cat sits on the mat. Is the word the a noun?","choices":["No, it is a helper word","Yes, it names a thing","Yes, it is a doing word"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the name anything by itself?","explain":"The is a helper word. It points to a noun, like the cat or the mat, but it is not a noun."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} ducks swim in the pond. Which word is the verb?","choices":["swim","ducks","pond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the doing word. What do the ducks do?","explain":"Swim is the verb. It tells what the {n} ducks do. Ducks and pond are nouns.","params":{"n":[2,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word is a noun?","choices":["table","hop","sing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try the test: which one sounds right after the?","explain":"Table is a noun. It names a thing, and the table sounds right. Hop and sing are doing words."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Dad and Ali walk to the park {n} times a week. Which word is a verb?","choices":["walk","park","Dad"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word tells what they do?","explain":"Walk is the verb. It tells what Dad and Ali do {n} times a week. Park and Dad are nouns.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Is the word school a noun?","choices":["Yes, it names a place","No, you cannot hold it","No, it is a doing word"],"answer":0,"hint":"A noun can name a person, a place or a thing.","explain":"School names a place, so it is a noun. A noun does not have to be something you can hold."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The frog jumps {n} times. Which word tells what the frog does?","choices":["jumps","frog","The"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: what does the frog do?","explain":"Jumps is the verb: the frog jumps {n} times. Frog is the noun that says who.","params":{"n":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A noun is a naming word. Girl, dog, ball and tree are nouns. A noun can name a person, a place or a thing. Mum is a person. A park is a place. A cup is a thing.","A verb is a doing word. Run, jump, eat and sing are verbs. Sleep is a verb too. You do it, even when you lie still!","Not sure? Try two tests. Say the word after the. The cake sounds right, so cake is a noun. Now say I can and the word. I can swim sounds right, so swim is a verb.","Put them together to make a sentence. Who is it? The dog. What does it do? It barks. The dog barks. Start with a capital letter. End with a full stop."],"adult":["In the first year of school, children learn that words do different jobs. Nouns name people, places and things (teacher, school, bike), and verbs name actions or states (run, eat, sleep). Using these words correctly in speech comes first; spotting them in a short written sentence comes next. The US Common Core asks for this in kindergarten, and the English national curriculum teaches the terms noun and verb by Year 2.","Two quick tests help a five or six year old sort a word. If it fits after the or a, it is probably a noun: the cake, a bed. If it fits after I can or they, it is probably a verb: I can read, they sing. Children then build the simplest sentence pattern, a noun phrase followed by a verb, such as The cat sleeps.","Common mistakes to watch for: thinking a noun must be something you can hold, so park or school are left out; thinking a verb must involve movement, so sleep or think are left out; calling the a noun; and putting the verb first, as in The sleeps cat. Some words can be both, like a drink and I drink, so judge the word by its job in the sentence.","In the lab your child taps the nouns in a park picture and meets two doing-word tags, sorts nouns into person, place and thing, drags verbs onto Sam to make him run, jump, eat and sleep while a noun card does nothing, sorts words with the two tests, and builds three sentences that match a picture."]},"familyTip":"Play Doing or Naming at teatime: say a word such as spoon, stir or kitchen, and your child shouts naming or doing, then acts out the doing words. Point at something and ask, \"What is it? What does it do?\" to make a two-word sentence together, like the kettle boils.","keyIdea":"A noun is a naming word: it names a person, a place or a thing, like girl, park or cup. A verb is a doing word: it tells what someone or something does, like run, eat or sleep. A simple sentence often has a noun that says who, then a verb that says what they do: The dog barks.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Naming word or doing word? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🏷 Noun (naming)","🏃 Verb (doing)"],"items":[["apple",0],["teacher",0],["garden",0],["chair",0],["hop",1],["drink",1],["laugh",1],["sit",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnv","caption":"Flagship lab: find nouns in a park, sort person, place and thing, make Sam do verbs, test words and build sentences.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_VY3rBq8RyP":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The dog sleeps below the table. Which words tell where?","choices":["under the table","on the table","in the table"],"answer":0,"hint":"Below means the same as which word?","explain":"Under the table. Under means below."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is the opposite of on?","choices":["off","in","up"],"answer":0,"hint":"You put a hat on. Then you take it ...","explain":"Off is the opposite of on. You put a hat on, then take it off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia stands at the side of her friend. Where is she?","choices":["next to her friend","under her friend","in her friend"],"answer":0,"hint":"At the side means the same as which words?","explain":"Next to her friend. Next to means at the side."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The cat jumps down from the wall. Which words tell which way?","choices":["off the wall","on the wall","in the wall"],"answer":0,"hint":"Moving down and away from the top is which word?","explain":"Off the wall. Off means moving down and away from the top of something."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} children walk ___ school in the morning. Which word fits?","choices":["to","from","off"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the morning they are going towards school.","explain":"The {n} children walk to school. To means going towards it.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Put the ball in the box. Where does it go?","choices":["Inside the box","On top of the box","Beside the box"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the little word in means.","explain":"Inside the box. In means inside. On top would be on, and beside would be next to."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} fish swim ___ the net and get away. Which word fits?","choices":["out of","into","on"],"answer":0,"hint":"They get away, so are they going inside or leaving?","explain":"The {n} fish swim out of the net. Out of means leaving the inside.","params":{"n":[2,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The book sits on top of the table. Which words tell where?","choices":["on the table","in the table","under the table"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the book on top, inside, or below?","explain":"On the table. On means on top. In is inside, and under is below."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} socks inside the drawer. Where are the socks?","choices":["in the drawer","on the drawer","next to the drawer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Inside means the same as which little word?","explain":"The {n} socks are in the drawer. In means inside.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is the opposite of in?","choices":["out","on","to"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of going in a door, then the other way.","explain":"Out is the opposite of in. You go in the door, then you come out."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} ducks walk ___ the pond and splash. Which word fits?","choices":["into","out of","under"],"answer":0,"hint":"Splash means they go in the water.","explain":"The {n} ducks walk into the pond. Into means going inside it.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some little words tell where things are. The cat is on the mat. The ball is under the bed. The fish is in the bowl.","Next to means at the side. The cup is next to the plate. They sit side by side.","Some little words tell which way to go. The frog hops off the log. It jumps into the pond. Then it hops to the flower.","Some of these words are opposites. In and out. On and off. Up and down. To and from."],"adult":["A preposition links a noun to the rest of a sentence, usually to show place, direction or time. US Common Core asks kindergarteners to use the most frequent prepositions (to, from, in, out, on, off, for, of, by, with) and first graders to use frequently occurring prepositions such as during, beyond and toward. In England, children meet position and direction language in Year 1 maths and use prepositions in writing from Year 3.","A preposition plus its noun group is a prepositional phrase: 'under the bed', 'into the pond'. Young children understand these from action first, so moving real objects to follow a spoken phrase is the best practice. Contrast pairs (in versus on, to versus from) build precision faster than single words.","Common mistakes: mixing up in and on (in means inside, on means on top of a surface); using next to and behind as if they meant the same; thinking 'off' only means a switch, not moving down from something; and mixing up to and from, which point opposite ways. Say the phrase and act it out to check.","In the lab your child drags a cat on, under, in and next to; names where a ball is; matches opposite pairs; hops a frog off a log, into a pond, out of it and to a flower; and follows tidy-up instructions."]},"familyTip":"Play 'Teddy says': ask your child to put a teddy on the chair, under the table, in a box and next to the door. Then swap roles so your child gives you the instructions.","keyIdea":"Prepositions are little words that tell where something is or which way it goes. Position words such as on, under, in and next to say where a thing is. Direction words such as to, from, into, out of and off say which way it moves. Many come in opposite pairs: in and out, on and off, up and down, to and from.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each word tell where it is, or which way it goes? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📍 Where it is","➡️ Which way it goes"],"items":[["on the mat",0],["under the bed",0],["in the box",0],["next to the cup",0],["into the pond",1],["off the log",1],["to the park",1],["out of the car",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagprep","caption":"Flagship lab: move the cat, say where the ball is, match opposites, hop the frog and tidy the room.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_18qkgxr_-T":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"One wish. Two ...?","choices":["wishes","wishs","wish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Wish ends in sh. Clap it: how many beats?","explain":"Wishes. Wish ends in sh, so it takes -es: wish-es, two beats."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"One hen. {n} ...?","choices":["hens","henes","hen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hen ends in n. Does it hiss or buzz?","explain":"{n} hens. Hen just takes -s. Hens has one beat.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"There is 1 ... on the plate. Which word fits?","choices":["apple","apples","applees"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many are there? One thing needs no ending.","explain":"1 apple. There is only one, so the word has no ending."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"One bus. {n} ...?","choices":["buses","buss","bus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bus already ends in s. What do s words take?","explain":"{n} buses. Bus ends in s, so we add -es. Bus-es has two beats.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word has an extra beat when you say it?","choices":["foxes","frogs","cats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap each one. Which one needs two claps?","explain":"Foxes: fox-es, two claps. Frogs and cats are one clap, so they only take -s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Plural means ...","choices":["more than one","just one","a word that ends in s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of one cat and two cats. Which one is plural?","explain":"Plural means more than one. A word can end in s and still be just one thing, like bus."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In 'Two kids ride the bus', which word is a plural noun?","choices":["kids","bus","ride"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the thing there is more than one of.","explain":"Kids is the plural: there are two of them. Bus ends in s but is one bus, and ride is a doing word."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"One dog. Two ...?","choices":["dogs","doges","dog"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dog ends in g. Does it hiss or buzz at the end?","explain":"Dogs. Dog does not end in a hiss or buzz sound, so it just takes -s."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"One box. {n} ...?","choices":["boxes","boxs","box"],"answer":0,"hint":"Box ends in x. Say the plural and clap the beats.","explain":"{n} boxes. Box ends in x, so we add -es. Box-es has two beats.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word means more than one?","choices":["cups","bus","glass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two of the words end in s but name just one thing.","explain":"Cups means more than one cup. Bus and glass end in s, but each is one thing."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam has {n} brush... Which ending?","choices":["-es: brushes","-s: brushs","no ending: brush"],"answer":0,"hint":"Brush ends in sh. What do sh words take?","explain":"{n} brushes. Brush ends in sh, so it takes -es, and you hear an extra beat.","params":{"n":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A noun is a word for a thing. Cat is a noun. Box is a noun.","One cat. Two cats. When there is more than one, we add s to the end. More than one is called plural.","Some words end in a hiss or a buzz sound, like fox, bus, wish and watch. They get es: foxes, buses, wishes, watches. Say fox-es and clap. You hear one more beat.","Bus ends in s, but it is just one bus. One thing gets no s. One apple. Three apples."],"adult":["Plural nouns name more than one thing. In England, Year 1 pupils learn the regular plural noun suffixes -s and -es (dog, dogs; wish, wishes), and the spelling rule is taught by sound: if the ending is heard as /s/ or /z/ it is spelt -s; if it is /ɪz/ and adds an extra syllable, it is spelt -es. US Common Core asks kindergarteners to form regular plurals orally by adding /s/ or /es/ (L.K.1c).","The -es words are the ones whose singular ends in a sibilant sound: s, ss, x, z, sh or ch (bus, kiss, box, buzz, dish, bench). Clapping syllables is the easiest check, because the -es always brings its own beat. Irregular plurals (mouse, mice; child, children) and the y to ies change come later, so leave them out for now.","Common mistakes: writing foxs or wishs because the child adds -s to everything; adding -es to words that do not hiss or buzz (penes); adding an ending when there is only one (1 apples); and thinking any word that ends in s is plural, such as bus or grass. Verbs like takes also end in s but are doing words, not plural nouns.","In the lab your child adds animals to a pen and sees the s appear, drags -s or -es onto six words, sorts plurals by one clap or two, finds the real plurals in sentences, and finishes a shop list where one thing gets no ending."]},"familyTip":"Play 'one, two' at tea time: you say 'one cup', your child says 'two cups'. Mix in hissing words like dish, glass and box, and clap together to hear the extra beat in dish-es.","keyIdea":"A noun names a thing. Singular means one; plural means more than one. Most nouns add -s to make the plural (cat, cats). Nouns that end in s, x, z, sh or ch add -es (fox, foxes; wish, wishes), and you can hear the extra beat. One thing gets no ending, and a word that ends in s, like bus, is not always plural.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each word take -s or -es to mean more than one? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Add -s","Add -es"],"items":[["cat",0],["dog",0],["hat",0],["bell",0],["fox",1],["dish",1],["bus",1],["watch",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagplural","caption":"Flagship lab: fill the pens, drag -s or -es, clap the beats, find the plurals and finish the shop list.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_uorNrPTh6U":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word gives a reason?","choices":["because","while","after"],"answer":0,"hint":"One word answers the question 'why?'.","explain":"Because gives a reason. While and after are time conjunctions."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"'Ben was tired, ___ he went to bed early.' Which word fits?","choices":["so","because","before"],"answer":0,"hint":"Being tired came first. Going to bed is what happened as a result.","explain":"So. 'So' comes in front of the result. 'Because' would mean going to bed made Ben tired."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is the best start for the last sentence of a story?","choices":["Finally,","First,","Next,"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which time adverb means the very last thing?","explain":"Finally means the last thing. First opens a story, and next moves it on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which sentence has the comma in the right place?","choices":["After the film ended, we went home.","After the film, ended we went home.","After, the film ended we went home."],"answer":0,"hint":"The comma goes at the end of the fronted clause.","explain":"'After the film ended, we went home.' The comma marks where the fronted clause stops."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Sam read {n} pages while he waited. What does 'while' tell us?","choices":["He read and waited at the same time","He read before he waited","He read because he waited"],"answer":0,"hint":"While is a time word. Does it mean before, after or at the same time?","explain":"While means at the same time. Sam read {n} pages during the wait.","params":{"n":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The match was stopped after {n} minutes because of the snow. What does 'because of the snow' tell us?","choices":["Why it was stopped","When it was stopped","Where it was stopped"],"answer":0,"hint":"Because of starts a phrase that gives a reason.","explain":"Why. 'Because of the snow' is the cause. 'After {n} minutes' is the phrase that tells when.","params":{"n":[10,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"'Ali packed his bag ___ he left for school.' Which word fits?","choices":["before","after","so"],"answer":0,"hint":"He needs his bag at school. Which happened first?","explain":"Before. Packing came first. 'After' would mean he packed once he had already gone."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word fits? 'The pond froze ___ it was so cold.'","choices":["because","so","before"],"answer":0,"hint":"The cold is the reason. Which word comes before a reason?","explain":"Because. The cold is the cause, and 'because' comes in front of the cause."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia waited {n} minutes. The bus was late. Which sentence joins these the right way?","choices":["Mia waited {n} minutes because the bus was late.","Mia waited {n} minutes so the bus was late.","The bus was late because Mia waited {n} minutes."],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the reason Mia waited? Put 'because' in front of the reason.","explain":"The late bus is the cause, so it goes after 'because': Mia waited {n} minutes because the bus was late.","params":{"n":[5,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which phrase tells WHEN?","choices":["during the match","under the table","because of the wind"],"answer":0,"hint":"Time phrases often start with during, before, after or on.","explain":"During the match tells when. Under the table tells where, and because of the wind tells why."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The cake baked for {n} minutes. Then we ate it. Which word fits? '___ the cake had baked for {n} minutes, we ate it.'","choices":["After","Before","Because"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which came first, the baking or the eating?","explain":"After. The baking came first, so 'After the cake had baked for {n} minutes, we ate it.'","params":{"n":[20,40]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words tell us when. Before, after, while and when are time words. 'Wash your hands before you eat.'","Some words tell us why. Because gives the reason. So gives what happened next. 'It was cold, so I wore a hat.' 'I wore a hat because it was cold.'","Take care. Swap because and so, and the sentence goes back to front. 'It was cold because I wore a hat' is silly!","Do not start each sentence with then. Use first, next, after that and finally. They help the reader follow the order. Short phrases can say when, where or why too: during the film, under the bed, because of the rain."],"adult":["In the English national curriculum, Year 3 pupils learn to express time, place and cause using conjunctions (when, before, after, while, so, because), adverbs (then, next, soon, therefore) and prepositions (before, after, during, in, because of). The grammar terms they meet are conjunction, clause, subordinate clause, adverb and preposition.","A conjunction such as because or after joins a main clause to a subordinate clause: 'We went inside because it was raining.' A time adverb links ideas across sentences: 'First, she opened the door. Then, she stepped outside.' A preposition starts a phrase: 'during the lesson', 'behind the shed', 'because of the noise'.","The commonest slip is swapping because and so. 'Because' comes before the cause; 'so' comes before the result, so swapping them reverses the meaning. Another is a 'then, then, then' story where every sentence starts the same way. A third is muddling before and after, which flips the order of events.","When a subordinate clause moves to the front ('After the bell rang, we lined up'), a comma marks where it ends. Pupils often put it in the wrong place. The lab practises each idea: joining clauses, ordering a story with time adverbs, sorting phrases into when, where and why, building cause-and-result sentences, and placing the comma."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, retell the day using first, next, after that and finally. Then play 'because or so': say half a sentence like 'I was hungry…' and take turns finishing it with 'so…' and then with 'because…'.","keyIdea":"Small joining words tell the reader when, where and why. Conjunctions like when, before, after and while show time; because and so show cause. Adverbs like first, next, soon and therefore link one sentence to the next. Prepositions start phrases like during the lesson (when), under the bridge (where) and because of the rain (why).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the bold word show time or cause? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["⏰ Time (when?)","❓ Cause (why?)"],"items":[["We lined up after the bell rang.",0],["Wash your hands before you eat.",0],["I hummed while I painted.",0],["Soon, the sun came out.",0],["I wore a coat because it was cold.",1],["It rained, so the match stopped.",1],["We stayed in because of the storm.",1],["He forgot his key. Therefore, he knocked.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwhen","caption":"Flagship lab: join clauses with conjunctions, fix a 'then, then' story, sort when, where and why phrases, and place a fronted clause's comma.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_N8CpN1EJrP":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does a sentence tell?","choices":["A whole idea","One word","Just a name"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about 'The dog runs.' It makes sense on its own.","explain":"A sentence tells a whole idea: who, and what they do. One word or a name is only a piece."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one is NOT a whole sentence?","choices":["The big blue ball","The ball rolls.","Kim kicks the ball."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one with no doing word.","explain":"'The big blue ball' names a thing, but it does not say what happens."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Make 'The frog' a whole sentence.","choices":["The frog hops.","The green frog","The frog the pond"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add a doing word that says what the frog does.","explain":"'hops' is a doing word, so 'The frog hops.' is whole. 'green' and 'the pond' do not say what it does."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"'The {n} birds sing in the tree.' How many sentences is this?","choices":["One","Two","None"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the who and the doing words.","explain":"One. 'in the tree' is a detail about where. There is one who (the {n} birds) and one doing word (sing), so one sentence.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which one is written the right way?","choices":["The sun is hot.","the sun is hot.","The sun is hot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the start and the end.","explain":"It starts with a capital letter (The) and ends with a full stop."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia makes {n} sentences. Each has a who. What else does each need?","choices":["A doing word","Another who","A picture"],"answer":0,"hint":"A sentence has a who, and then what they do.","explain":"Each of the {n} sentences needs a doing word, to say what the who does.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Add a detail to 'The dog runs.' Which is best?","choices":["The dog runs in the park.","The dog runs. In the park.","In the park."],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the detail inside the one sentence.","explain":"'The dog runs in the park.' keeps one sentence. 'In the park.' on its own is not finished."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is a whole sentence?","choices":["The cat naps.","The fat cat","naps on a rug"],"answer":0,"hint":"A whole sentence says who, and what they do.","explain":"'The cat naps.' says who (the cat) and what it does (naps). The others each miss a piece."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} ducks. 'The {n} ducks' is not finished. What is missing?","choices":["What the ducks do","Who it is about","Nothing is missing"],"answer":0,"hint":"We know who. Do we know what they do?","explain":"We know who: the {n} ducks. We do not know what they do, so add a doing word: The {n} ducks swim.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"'jumps in the mud' is not finished. What is missing?","choices":["Who jumps","A doing word","Nothing is missing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Jumps is the doing word. Who is doing it?","explain":"Jumps is the doing word, but we do not know who. Add a who: The pig jumps in the mud."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam has {n} sentences to write. Where does the full stop go in each one?","choices":["At the end","After the first word","At the start"],"answer":0,"hint":"The full stop tells you to stop.","explain":"A full stop goes at the end of each of the {n} sentences, after the last word.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Words join up to make a sentence. A sentence tells a whole idea. 'The dog runs.' is a sentence.","A sentence needs two parts. Who is it about? The dog. What does it do? Runs. Put them together: The dog runs.","'The dog' is not finished. You want to ask, what does the dog do? 'jumps high' is not finished too. Who jumps?","You can make a sentence bigger. The cat sleeps on the mat. The cat sleeps all day. It is still one sentence. When you write it, start with a big capital letter. Stop with a full stop at the end."],"adult":["In England, Year 1 pupils learn how words combine to make sentences, using the terms word, sentence, capital letter, full stop and punctuation; US Common Core asks kindergarteners to produce and expand complete sentences in shared language activities (L.K.1f). The grammar underneath is a subject (who or what) plus a verb (what they do), which together express a complete thought.","The quickest test is to say it aloud and listen: if you are left asking 'who?' or 'what happened?', it is a fragment, not a sentence. 'The big red bus' names a thing but has no verb; 'ran to the park' has a verb but no subject. Children can often hear this before they can explain it, so talk comes first and writing second.","Common mistakes: treating any group of words as a sentence because it starts with a capital letter; thinking a longer sentence must be two sentences, and putting a full stop before an added detail ('The cat sleeps. On the mat.'); full stops after every word or line; and a lower-case first letter. Expanding with a where, when or how phrase keeps one subject and one verb, so it stays one sentence.","In the lab your child couples a who car and a doing car to make word trains, sorts whole sentences from unfinished bits, mends three fragments with the missing piece, stretches 'The cat sleeps.' with details, and adds the capital letter and the full stop."]},"familyTip":"Play 'finish my sentence': say 'The cat…' and stop. Your child adds what the cat does. Swap: they start with a doing word ('…jumps!') and you add who. Then make it bigger together: where? when?","keyIdea":"A sentence is a group of words that tells a complete thought. It says who or what (the dog) and what they do (runs). A bit with a piece missing, like 'The dog' or 'jumps high', is not a whole sentence yet. You can add details about where, when or how, and it is still one sentence. A written sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a whole sentence, or not finished yet? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Whole sentence","🧩 Not finished"],"items":[["The dog barks.",0],["Sam reads.",0],["The ducks swim.",0],["Mum laughs.",0],["The little duck",1],["eats a cake",1],["in the box",1],["My red hat",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsent","caption":"Flagship lab: couple who and doing-word cars into sentences, sort whole from unfinished, fix fragments, add details and put in the capital letter and full stop.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0QJoKWABdC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?","choices":["Before breakfast, she packed her bag.","Before, breakfast she packed her bag.","Before breakfast she, packed her bag."],"answer":0,"hint":"The comma goes after the whole opener.","explain":"The opener is \"Before breakfast\", so the comma goes after breakfast. The other two split the opener or the subject and verb."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which part is the adverbial? \"A troll was waiting under the bridge.\"","choices":["under the bridge","A troll","was waiting"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part tells where the troll was?","explain":"\"Under the bridge\" tells where the troll waited, so it is the adverbial. \"A troll\" is the subject and \"was waiting\" is the verb."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which sentence needs no comma?","choices":["The fox ran home at midnight.","At midnight the fox ran home.","Quietly the fox ran home."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the sentence where the adverbial is at the end.","explain":"In \"The fox ran home at midnight\" the adverbial is at the end, so no comma is needed. The other two have fronted adverbials and need one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is wrong with \"The children, ran home after school.\"?","choices":["The comma splits the subject from its verb","It needs a comma after school","Nothing is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a fronted adverbial at the start?","explain":"There is no fronted adverbial, and a comma must not come between the children and ran."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A fronted adverbial has {n} words. How many commas go after it?","answer":"1","hint":"Short or long, the opener gets the same punctuation.","explain":"Whether it has {n} words or one, a fronted adverbial gets exactly 1 comma, straight after its last word.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which opener tells where?","choices":["In the dark cave,","Next week,","As fast as he could,"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one names a place you could point to?","explain":"\"In the dark cave\" names a place, so it tells where. \"Next week\" tells when and \"As fast as he could\" tells how."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam's story has {n} sentences that all start with \"The dog\". He fronts an adverbial in {k} of them. How many still start with \"The dog\"?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Each fronted adverbial gives one sentence a new start.","explain":"{n} take away {k} new openings leaves {=n-k} sentences still starting with \"The dog\".","params":{"n":[4,7],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Move the adverbial to the front: \"We went swimming after school.\"","choices":["After school, we went swimming.","after school, we went swimming.","After school we, went swimming."],"answer":0,"hint":"The opener gets the capital letter and a comma after it.","explain":"\"After school\" moves to the front with a capital A and a comma after it; \"we\" becomes lower case."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where does the comma go after the fronted adverbial in \"Later that day the children ran home\"?","choices":["After day","After Later","After children"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the whole opener that tells when. Where does it end?","explain":"The fronted adverbial is \"Later that day\", so the comma goes after day: Later that day, the children ran home."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A paragraph has {n} sentences. {k} of them start with a fronted adverbial. How many commas after fronted adverbials should it have?","answer":"k","hint":"Each fronted adverbial gets one comma after it. The other sentences do not need one for this rule.","explain":"Each of the {k} fronted adverbials needs one comma after it, so the paragraph needs {=k} of these commas, whatever the other {=n-k} sentences do.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Silently, the cat crept closer.\" What does the adverbial \"Silently\" tell us?","choices":["How the cat moved","When the cat moved","Where the cat moved"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: in what way did the cat creep?","explain":"Silently tells how the cat crept. A when adverbial would be like \"At dawn\", and a where one like \"Under the table\"."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Jo writes {n} sentences. She fronts the adverbial in {k} of them. How many still start with the subject?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the sentences with a fronted adverbial away from the total.","explain":"{n} sentences take away {k} with a fronted adverbial leaves {=n-k} that start with the subject.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some parts of a sentence tell when, where or how. At midnight tells when. Under the bridge tells where. Silently tells how. These parts are called adverbials. An adverbial can be one word or a group of words.","You can move an adverbial to the front: The fox crept through the garden at midnight. At midnight, the fox crept through the garden. Now it is a fronted adverbial. It starts the sentence, so it gets the capital letter.","Put a comma straight after the whole opener. Later that day, the children ran home. Not Later, that day. The comma shows the reader where the opener stops and the main part starts.","Fronted adverbials help you start sentences in new ways. Mix them up: After tea, Under the stairs, As fast as he could. If the adverbial stays at the end, you do not need a comma."],"adult":["In the English National Curriculum, fronted adverbials and the comma after them are Year 4 grammar and punctuation, listed in English Appendix 2 with the example 'Later that day, I heard the bad news.' Pupils learn the term adverbial and use fronted adverbials to vary sentence openings. US Common Core L.5.2b, using a comma to separate an introductory element from the rest of the sentence, covers the same rule.","An adverbial can be a single adverb (silently), a prepositional phrase (under the bridge, after lunch) or a subordinate clause (as fast as he could). Children often think only -ly words count. Ask what the chunk tells: when, where or how. If it answers one of those and can move, it is an adverbial.","Common mistakes: putting the comma inside the phrase ('Later, that day the children…'), leaving it out, and putting a comma between the subject and the verb ('The children, ran home'). Children also forget that the fronted word takes the capital letter. Be careful too with a page where every sentence now starts with a fronted adverbial; variety means mixing them with other openings.","In the lab your child taps the adverbial that tells when, where or how in three sentences, then drags an adverbial to the front and sees the capital letter and comma appear. They tap the correct comma gap in four sentences and judge six sentences as correct or needing a fix. Last, they sort six openers into when, where and how."]},"familyTip":"Take a plain sentence from a reading book, like \"We walked to the shop.\" Take turns adding a fronted adverbial and saying it with a pause where the comma goes: \"After breakfast, we walked to the shop.\" \"In the pouring rain, we walked to the shop.\"","keyIdea":"An adverbial is a word or phrase that tells when, where or how something happens: at midnight, under the bridge, silently. Moved to the start of a sentence, it becomes a fronted adverbial, and a comma goes straight after the whole of it: \"Later that day, the children ran home.\" The opener takes the capital letter. An adverbial left at the end needs no comma, and a comma never goes between the subject and its verb.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the comma right? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✓ Correct","✗ Needs fixing"],"items":[["At the weekend, we visited Gran.",0],["Carefully, she opened the box.",0],["As the sun set, the owls woke up.",0],["We visited Gran at the weekend.",0],["At the weekend we visited Gran.",1],["Carefully she, opened the box.",1],["As the sun, set the owls woke up.",1],["The owls, woke up as the sun set.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfront","caption":"Flagship lab: find the adverbial, drag it to the front, place the comma, judge sentences and sort openers into when, where and how.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QEr24lqzvH":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What goes at the start of every sentence?","choices":["A capital letter","A full stop","A question mark"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the big T in The cat sat on the mat.","explain":"Every sentence starts with a capital letter. The marks go at the end."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which mark ends this sentence? Can you see the bird","choices":["?","!","."],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it tell, ask or shout?","explain":"Can you see the bird asks something, so it ends with a question mark ?"},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where does the full stop go? I like red apples","choices":["After apples","After like","After red"],"answer":0,"hint":"The full stop goes where the idea is finished.","explain":"After apples, the last word. I like. is not a finished idea, so a full stop after like is too soon."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which line has two sentences written the right way?","choices":["It was hot. We had ice cream.","It was hot we had ice cream.","It was hot. we had ice cream."],"answer":0,"hint":"Each idea needs a full stop and the next one a capital letter.","explain":"It was hot. We had ice cream. has a full stop after hot and a capital W for the new sentence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which mark ends this sentence? Help, the boat is sinking","choices":["!","?","."],"answer":0,"hint":"How would you say it if it were true?","explain":"Help, the boat is sinking is a shout with a big feeling, so it ends with an exclamation mark !"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A story has {a} telling sentences, {b} questions and 1 exclamation. How many full stops does it have?","answer":"a","hint":"Questions get ? and the exclamation gets !. Only telling sentences end with a full stop.","explain":"Only the {a} telling sentences end with a full stop, so there are {a} full stops.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam wrote {n} sentences. He forgot the capital letter on 1 of them. How many sentences start the right way?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take away the one with no capital letter.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}, so {=n-1} sentences start with a capital letter.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which word needs a capital letter? the dog ate my hat.","choices":["the","dog","hat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word comes first? The first word gets the capital.","explain":"the is the first word, so it needs a capital: The dog ate my hat."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which sentence is written the right way?","choices":["The dog ran fast.","the dog ran fast.","The dog ran fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first letter and the very end.","explain":"The dog ran fast. starts with a capital T and ends with a full stop, so it is right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ali writes {a} telling sentences and {b} asking sentences. How many question marks does he need?","answer":"b","hint":"Only the asking sentences end with a question mark.","explain":"Only the {b} asking sentences need a question mark. The {a} telling ones end with full stops. So he needs {b}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which mark ends this sentence? What a huge cake that is","choices":["!","?","."],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it asking something, or showing surprise?","explain":"It starts with What, but it does not ask. It shows surprise, so it ends with an exclamation mark !"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia writes {n} sentences. How many capital letters does she need at the start of her sentences?","answer":"n","hint":"Every sentence starts with one capital letter.","explain":"Each sentence starts with one capital letter, so {n} sentences need {n} capitals at the start.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A sentence starts with a big letter. It is called a capital letter. The cat sat on the mat. See the big T?","A sentence ends with a mark. It shows the idea is done. A dot is called a full stop. Put it after the last word, not in the middle.","Some sentences ask. Can you see the moon? Asking ends with a question mark. Telling ends with a full stop. Some shout or feel big. What a big cake this is! Ouch, that hurts! These end with an exclamation mark.","Two ideas need two sentences. We went to the park. We fed the ducks. Stop after park. Then start with a capital."],"adult":["This is sentence demarcation. In England's National Curriculum, Year 1 pupils begin to punctuate sentences using a capital letter and a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark, and use a capital letter for names and the pronoun I. Year 2 adds the four sentence forms: statement, question, exclamation and command. US Common Core L.K.2 and L.1.2 ask for the same capitals and end punctuation.","Teach the marks by what the sentence does, not by its length. A statement tells, a question asks and needs an answer, and an exclamation shows strong feeling or surprise. In the grammar tests used in England, an exclamation sentence starts with What or How and has a verb, as in What a big cake this is! Short cries such as Ouch! and commands such as Stop! may also take an exclamation mark.","Common mistakes: no capital at the start, a capital in the middle of a sentence, a full stop at the end of a line rather than at the end of the idea, a full stop on a question, and two ideas run together with no full stop. Another slip is reading What a big cake this is as a question because it starts with What.","In the lab your child taps the first word to give it a capital, drags a full stop to the end of the idea, picks a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark by how each sentence sounds, and splits squashed lines into two sentences."]},"familyTip":"When you read together, stop at each full stop and take a breath, and make your voice go up at a question mark. Then ask your child: how should this one end?","keyIdea":"Every sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a mark. A telling sentence (a statement) ends with a full stop. An asking sentence (a question) ends with a question mark. A sentence that shows surprise, a big feeling or a shout ends with an exclamation mark. When two ideas are squashed together, put a full stop after the first one and a capital letter at the start of the next.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How does each sentence end? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🙂 Telling: full stop .","🤔 Asking: question mark ?","😮 Big feeling: exclamation mark !"],"items":[["The sun is hot",0],["I have a red coat",0],["Can you swim",1],["Where is Dad",1],["Is it time for tea",1],["What a big dog you have",2],["Ouch, my toe",2],["We went to the park",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstop","caption":"Flagship lab: give sentences a capital letter, drag full stops, pick . ? or ! by how a sentence sounds, and split squashed lines into two sentences.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YXVQaufkKO":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What job does 'and' do?","choices":["It joins words and ideas","It ends a sentence","It asks a question"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of 'cats and dogs'. What does 'and' do there?","explain":"'And' is a joining word, a conjunction. It joins two things or two ideas."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which list is written the right way?","choices":["I ate eggs, toast and jam.","I ate eggs and toast and and jam.","I ate eggs, toast, jam and."],"answer":0,"hint":"Commas between things. 'And' just before the last one.","explain":"Eggs, toast and jam: a comma between the first two, and 'and' before the last thing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Join 'I got up.' and 'I had my breakfast.'","choices":["I got up and I had my breakfast.","I got up and i had my breakfast.","I got up. And I had my breakfast and."],"answer":0,"hint":"Does 'I' ever get a small letter?","explain":"One full stop at the end, and 'I' always keeps its capital letter, even in the middle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which ideas go together?","choices":["I was hungry and I ate a sandwich.","I was hungry and the sky is blue.","I was hungry and the car is fast."],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the second idea have something to do with the first?","explain":"Being hungry and eating a sandwich go together. The other endings have nothing to do with being hungry."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is easiest to read?","choices":["We went to the zoo. We saw a lion and it roared.","We went to the zoo and we saw a lion and it roared and we went home and we slept.","We went to the zoo we saw a lion it roared."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for short sentences with a full stop at the end of each.","explain":"Too many ands make one long chain. Short sentences, with one 'and' at most, are easy to follow."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A sentence has {n} ands in a long chain. You change all but one of them into full stops. How many ands are left?","answer":"1","hint":"All but one means you keep just one.","explain":"You change {=n-1} of the {n} ands into full stops, so 1 'and' is left.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You write {n} short sentences. Then you join two of them with 'and'. How many sentences are there now?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Two sentences become one, so you have one fewer.","explain":"Joining two sentences turns 2 into 1, so {n} sentences become {=n-1}.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Where does 'and' go in a list of four things?","choices":["Just before the last thing","Between every thing","Right at the start"],"answer":0,"hint":"The other gaps get commas.","explain":"Commas go between the first things, and 'and' goes just before the last one: red, blue, green and pink."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where does 'and' go? I like ___ apples ___ pears ___.","choices":["I like apples and pears.","I like and apples pears.","I like apples pears and."],"answer":0,"hint":"'And' joins two things. Put it between them.","explain":"'And' sits between the two things it joins: apples and pears."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Zara joins {n} pairs of short sentences with 'and'. Each pair becomes one sentence. How many full stops are there now?","answer":"n","hint":"Each joined sentence has one full stop, at the end.","explain":"Each pair becomes one sentence with one full stop, so {n} pairs make {n} sentences and {n} full stops.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Join 'The sun came out.' and 'We went to the park.'","choices":["The sun came out and we went to the park.","The sun came out. and we went to the park.","The sun came and out we went to the park."],"answer":0,"hint":"Take out the first full stop. Put 'and' where it was.","explain":"The first full stop goes, 'and' joins the two ideas, and 'We' becomes 'we' because it is not the start any more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A list has {n} things in it. How many commas does it need, with 'and' before the last thing?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Count the gaps between things. The last gap gets 'and', not a comma.","explain":"{n} things have {=n-1} gaps. The last gap gets 'and', so {=n-2} gaps get a comma.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["'And' is a joining word. It joins two things. I like cats and dogs. 'And' goes in the middle, between the two things.","'And' can join two sentences. The dog barked. The cat ran. Now make one: The dog barked and the cat ran. One sentence has one full stop, at the end.","In a list, put a comma between the things. Put 'and' just before the last one. I packed a hat, a coat and a scarf.","Join ideas that go together. I was cold and I put on my coat. Do not use too many ands. If it gets long, stop and start a new sentence."],"adult":["The English national curriculum (Appendix 2) asks Year 1 pupils to join words and join clauses using 'and'. Their terms are word, sentence, full stop and capital letter. Year 2 adds co-ordination with 'or', 'and' and 'but', and commas to separate items in a list, with the terms statement and comma. The word conjunction is a pupil term from Year 3. Before that, 'joining word' is the usual name.","'And' is a co-ordinating conjunction. It links two words, two phrases or two main clauses of equal weight. When two sentences become one, the first full stop goes. The next word loses its capital letter, unless it is 'I' or a name. In a list of three or more things, school style puts commas between the items and 'and' before the last one. A comma before that last 'and' (the Oxford comma) is optional.","Common mistakes: putting 'and' before both items or at the end ('and cats dogs'); leaving the first full stop in ('I ran. and I jumped'); writing a small 'i' in the middle; using 'and' between every item in a list; joining ideas with no link; and the very common chain of 'and then… and then… and then', where one sentence runs on and on.","In the lab your child drags 'and' between two things and joins two sentences, then sees the full stop go. They build lists with a comma and an 'and', and pick the idea that fits after 'and'. Last, they tap extra ands to turn a long chain into short sentences."]},"familyTip":"Tell each other about your day in two short sentences, then join them with 'and'. When a story turns into 'and then… and then…', clap where a new sentence should start.","keyIdea":"'And' is a joining word (a conjunction). It goes between two things to join them: cats and dogs. It can join two sentences into one longer sentence: 'The dog barked and the cat ran.' The full stop in the middle goes, and the second part starts with a small letter, except 'I' and names. In a list of three or more things, commas go between the things and 'and' goes only before the last one: a hat, a coat and a scarf. 'And' joins ideas that go together, and a long chain of ands is hard to follow, so start a new sentence instead.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is 'and' used the right way? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Joined the right way","🔧 Needs fixing"],"items":[["I like cats and dogs.",0],["I like and cats dogs.",1],["We swam and we had chips.",0],["We swam. and we had chips.",1],["I see a cat, a dog and a hen.",0],["I see a cat and, a dog a hen.",1],["It rained and we got wet.",0],["It rained and my pen is red.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagand","caption":"Flagship lab: put 'and' between two things, join two sentences, build lists, pick ideas that go together and break up a chain of ands.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4-vfMgmCVB":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Leo fed the dog. Leo took the dog for a walk. Which is the most cohesive second sentence?","choices":["Then he took it for a walk.","Then Leo took the dog for a walk.","Then it took him for a walk."],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep Leo and the dog in the first sentence. Then point back to them.","explain":"He points back to Leo and it points back to the dog, so nothing is repeated and the meaning stays clear."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word is a synonym that could replace the second 'the volcano'?","choices":["the mountain","the town","the lava"],"answer":0,"hint":"A synonym must name the very same thing, not something nearby.","explain":"The mountain names the same volcano in a new way. The town and the lava are different things."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A paragraph names the fox {n} times. You keep the noun at the first mention only. How many times can you use a pronoun or synonym instead?","answer":"n-1","hint":"The first mention stays as the noun. What about all the others?","explain":"The first mention keeps the noun as the antecedent, so the other {=n-1} mentions can be pronouns or synonyms.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Penguins are birds. ___ they cannot fly. Which adverbial fits?","choices":["However,","As a result,","Firstly,"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is not flying a result of being a bird, or a surprise?","explain":"Most birds fly, so penguins not flying is a contrast. However shows the contrast."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mo gave Sam a pen. He smiled. Why is this sentence unclear?","choices":["He could mean Mo or Sam.","Smiled is spelt wrong.","It needs a comma."],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask who the pronoun points back to. Is there more than one choice?","explain":"There are two boys, so he is ambiguous. Use the name, for example 'Sam smiled', to keep the chain clear."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A recipe has {n} steps. Firstly and Finally are used once each. Next and After that fill the rest. How many steps use Next or After that?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Firstly takes one step and Finally takes one. Count what is left.","explain":"Firstly and Finally take 2 of the {n} steps, leaving {=n-2} for Next or After that.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Some animals sleep through winter. ___ hedgehogs hibernate from November. Which adverbial fits?","choices":["For example,","However,","As a result,"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are hedgehogs a contrast, a result, or one case of the idea?","explain":"Hedgehogs are one case of animals that sleep through winter, so for example is the right link."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which sentence breaks the chain? (1) Bats hunt at night. (2) They use sound to find insects. (3) She went to the shop.","choices":["Sentence 3","Sentence 2","Sentence 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a pronoun that does not point back to anything.","explain":"She has no antecedent in the paragraph, so sentence 3 does not link back. Sentence 2 links with they."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Owls hunt at night. Owls have soft feathers. Which is the best way to write the second sentence?","choices":["They have soft feathers.","Owls have soft feathers.","It has soft feathers."],"answer":0,"hint":"Owls is plural. Which pronoun points back to more than one?","explain":"They points back to owls, so we do not repeat the noun. It would be for one thing only."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A paragraph has {n} sentences. Every sentence after the first starts with a linking adverbial. How many linking adverbials are there?","answer":"n-1","hint":"The first sentence has nothing before it to link back to.","explain":"Only the {=n-1} sentences after the first link back, so there are {=n-1} linking adverbials.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"It rained all morning. ___ the match was called off. Which adverbial fits?","choices":["As a result,","However,","For example,"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the rain cause something, or is this a surprise?","explain":"The rain caused the match to be called off, so as a result shows the cause and effect."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Instructions have {n} steps. Step 1 starts with Firstly and the last step starts with Finally. How many steps are left for words like Next or After that?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take away the first step and the last step from the total.","explain":"Two of the {n} steps already have Firstly and Finally, so {=n-2} steps are left for Next or After that.","params":{"n":[4,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A good paragraph hangs together like a chain. Each sentence links back to the one before. Writers use small tools to make these links. They are called cohesive devices.","One tool is a pronoun. Foxes live in towns. They hunt at night. The word they points back to foxes. Keep the noun the first time, so the reader knows who they means. If he could mean two people, use the name again.","Another tool is a synonym, a new name for the same thing. The volcano erupted. Ash from the mountain fell on the town. Now you do not say volcano twice.","Adverbials link ideas too. Firstly, next and finally show the order of steps. As a result shows cause. However shows a contrast. For example gives one case. Pick the one that matches how the ideas connect."],"adult":["Cohesion is how the sentences in a paragraph link to each other. The National Curriculum for England asks pupils in Years 5 and 6 to use a wide range of devices to build cohesion within a paragraph, such as then, after that, this and firstly. In Year 6 this widens to repetition, ellipsis and adverbials such as on the other hand or as a consequence.","The main devices are reference and linking. A pronoun or a synonym refers back to an earlier noun, its antecedent, which forms a reference chain. Adverbials of time and sequence order events or steps. Adverbials of cause, contrast and example show the logic between two ideas. A fronted adverbial often carries the link, followed by a comma.","Watch for three common mistakes. One is an ambiguous pronoun: in 'Mo gave Sam a book and he smiled', he could mean either boy. Another is swapping the first mention for a pronoun, so the chain has no anchor. A third is a linking adverbial that does not match the logic, such as however before a result.","In the lab, children tap a repeated noun to replace it with a pronoun and choose a synonym that names the same thing. They drag adverbials of sequence into a set of instructions and pick cause, contrast or example links. Finally they find the sentence that breaks the chain."]},"familyTip":"When your child reads you a paragraph they wrote, point at a he, she, it or they and ask \"Who or what does this point back to?\" If the answer could be two people, help them put the name back in.","keyIdea":"A cohesive paragraph links each sentence back to the one before. Pronouns and synonyms refer back to a noun the reader has already met, called the antecedent, so keep the noun at its first mention. Adverbials of sequence such as firstly, next, after that and finally show order. Adverbials such as as a result, however and for example show cause, contrast and example. Choose the adverbial that matches how the two ideas really connect.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which cohesive device? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Points back (pronoun)","Shows order (sequence)","Shows logic (cause, contrast)"],"items":[["they",0],["it",0],["she",0],["Firstly",1],["After that",1],["Finally",1],["As a result",2],["However",2],["Therefore",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcohe","caption":"Flagship lab: swap repeats for pronouns and synonyms, order steps, link ideas, find the break.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_B8JOz79O6t":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one is a clause?","choices":["the baby laughed","in the morning","a red kite"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a doing word, a verb. A clause has one; a phrase does not.","explain":"Laughed is a verb, so \"the baby laughed\" is a clause. The other two have no verb, so they are phrases."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is a full sentence?","choices":["When the film ended, we went home.","When the film ended.","After the film."],"answer":0,"hint":"A full sentence needs a main clause.","explain":"Only the first has a main clause, \"we went home\". The others are fragments."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In \"If it is sunny, we will go to the park\", which part is the subordinate clause?","choices":["If it is sunny","we will go to the park","to the park"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part leaves you waiting?","explain":"\"If it is sunny\" cannot stand alone, so it is the subordinate clause, even though it comes first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In \"She smiled after she won\", what job does \"after\" do?","choices":["Conjunction","Preposition","Verb"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a verb in the words after it?","explain":"\"she won\" has the verb won, so it is a clause. After joins it on, so it is a conjunction."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"In \"She smiled after the race\", what job does \"after\" do?","choices":["Preposition","Conjunction","Verb"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a verb in the words after it?","explain":"\"the race\" has no verb, so it is a phrase. Here after is a preposition."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word joins two equal main clauses?","choices":["but","because","if"],"answer":0,"hint":"Coordinating conjunctions join equals.","explain":"But is a coordinating conjunction. Because and if start subordinate clauses."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You join {n} main clauses into one sentence, putting \"and\" between each pair. How many times do you write \"and\"?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Draw the clauses in a row and count the gaps between them.","explain":"{n} clauses in a row have {=n-1} gaps, so you write and {=n-1} times.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A story has {a} subordinate clauses. Each one needs its own main clause to make a sentence. How many main clauses do you need at least?","answer":"a","hint":"Pair each subordinate clause with one main clause.","explain":"One main clause for each subordinate clause means at least {a} main clauses.","params":{"a":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In \"We stayed in because it rained\", which part is the main clause?","choices":["We stayed in","because it rained","it rained because"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part makes sense as a sentence on its own?","explain":"\"We stayed in.\" makes sense alone, so it is the main clause. \"Because it rained\" is a subordinate clause that leaves you waiting."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Each clause has one verb. A sentence has {n} verbs, each in its own clause. How many clauses does it have?","answer":"n","hint":"One verb means one clause.","explain":"With one verb in each clause, {n} verbs means {n} clauses.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word is a subordinating conjunction?","choices":["although","but","or"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try the flip test. Which word can move to the front with its clause?","explain":"\"Although it was cold, we played out\" still works, so although is subordinating. But and or are coordinating."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A long sentence has {n} clauses. Only one of them is the main clause. How many subordinate clauses does it have?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the one main clause away from the total.","explain":"{n} clauses take away 1 main clause leaves {=n-1} subordinate clauses.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A clause is a group of words with a verb in it. The verb is the doing or being word. The dog barked is a clause, because barked is the verb. After lunch has no verb, so it is a phrase.","Some clauses make sense on their own. We stayed in. That is a main clause. Other clauses leave you waiting. Because it rained... what happened? That is a subordinate clause. It needs a main clause to lean on.","Conjunctions join clauses. And, but, or and so join two main clauses that are equal. Because, when, if and although start a subordinate clause. Try the flip test: Because it rained, we stayed in. It still works, so because is subordinating.","Watch out for words like after and until. In after we ate, a verb comes next, so after is a conjunction. In after lunch, there is no verb, so after is a preposition."],"adult":["The English National Curriculum lists clause, subordinate clause, conjunction and preposition as Year 3 grammar terms in English Appendix 2, building on Year 2 work on co-ordination (or, and, but) and subordination (when, if, that, because). The US Common Core standard L.3.1h asks pupils to use coordinating and subordinating conjunctions, L.3.1i to produce simple, compound and complex sentences, and L.4.1f to correct fragments.","Teach the tests rather than lists. Has it got a verb? Then it is a clause. Can it stand alone? Then it is a main clause. Can the conjunction and its clause move to the front? Then the conjunction is subordinating, and a comma follows the fronted clause. Coordinating conjunctions such as but and so fail that flip test.","Common mistakes: writing a subordinate clause alone as a sentence ('Because the bus was late.'), thinking the clause that comes first must be the main one, and calling every joining word an 'and word'. Pupils also confuse prepositions with conjunctions, since after, before and until can be both. The word that follows decides: a clause makes it a conjunction, a phrase makes it a preposition.","In the lab your child taps the verb to tell clauses from phrases, picks the main clause in three sentences, mends three fragments by adding a main clause, uses the flip test to sort four conjunctions as coordinating or subordinating, and decides whether after and until are conjunctions or prepositions."]},"familyTip":"Say a subordinate clause like \"When I got home\" and stop. Your child adds a main clause to finish it: \"When I got home, I ate a snack.\" Then swap roles and try the flip test together.","keyIdea":"A clause is a group of words with a verb: the dog barked. A phrase has no verb: after lunch. A main clause makes sense on its own. A subordinate clause, such as because it rained, leaves you waiting and needs a main clause. Conjunctions join clauses. Coordinating conjunctions (and, but, or, so) join two equal clauses. Subordinating conjunctions (because, when, if, although) start a subordinate clause, which can often move to the front with a comma.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Coordinating or subordinating? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["↔ Coordinating (joins equals)","↪ Subordinating (starts a subordinate clause)"],"items":[["and",0],["but",0],["or",0],["so",0],["because",1],["when",1],["if",1],["although",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagclause","caption":"Flagship lab: tell clauses from phrases, find main clauses, fix fragments, flip-test conjunctions and spot when after is a preposition.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_u1-UfD0rTH":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In 'Please shut the door.' which word is the bossy verb?","choices":["shut","Please","door"],"answer":0,"hint":"The bossy verb is the doing word. What are you told to do?","explain":"You are told to shut the door, so shut is the bossy verb. Please is a polite word and door is a thing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What type of sentence is 'How kind you are!'?","choices":["Exclamation","Question","Command"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it ask something, or show a big feeling?","explain":"It starts with How but does not ask anything. It shows a big feeling and ends with !, so it is an exclamation."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Turn 'The cat is asleep.' into a question.","choices":["Is the cat asleep?","The cat is asleep?","Cat, be asleep."],"answer":0,"hint":"Move the verb is to the front, then change the end mark.","explain":"Moving is to the front and ending with a question mark makes Is the cat asleep? Just adding ? does not change the order."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia wrote {s} statements and {c} commands. Each ends with a full stop. How many full stops did she write?","answer":"s+c","hint":"Statements and these commands both end with a full stop. Add them.","explain":"{s} + {c} = {=s+c} full stops, one at the end of each sentence.","params":{"s":[2,7],"c":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word can start an exclamation sentence?","choices":["What","Is","Can"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of What a big dog that is!","explain":"An exclamation starts with What or How. Is and Can start questions, such as Is it big?"},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What type of sentence is 'Wash your hands.'?","choices":["Command","Statement","Question"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it tell you something, or tell you to do something?","explain":"Wash is a bossy verb telling you what to do, so it is a command, even though it ends with a full stop."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which mark should end 'Where is my coat'?","choices":["?",".","!"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is a question word. What does the sentence do?","explain":"It asks something, so it is a question and ends with a question mark: Where is my coat?"},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Make 'You close the gate.' into a command.","choices":["Close the gate.","You close the gate?","What a gate!"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take away the word that says who. What word comes first then?","explain":"Taking away You leaves the bossy verb first: Close the gate. That is a command."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Zoe wrote {c} commands. Each command has one bossy verb. She adds {k} more commands. How many bossy verbs now?","answer":"c+k","hint":"Every command needs its own bossy verb. Add the commands together.","explain":"{c} + {k} = {=c+k} commands, and each has one bossy verb, so there are {=c+k} bossy verbs.","params":{"c":[2,6],"k":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Four types of sentences: 'Sit down!' ends with an exclamation mark. Is it a statement, question, exclamation or command?","choices":["Command","Exclamation","Question"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at its job, not only its end mark. Does it tell someone to do something?","explain":"Sit is a bossy verb telling someone what to do, so it is a command. A command can end with ! when it is said strongly."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ben wrote {q} questions and {s} statements. How many question marks did he need?","answer":"q","hint":"Only one type of sentence ends with a question mark.","explain":"Each question ends with a question mark, so he needs {q}. The {s} statements end with full stops.","params":{"q":[2,9],"s":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sentence is a question?","choices":["Can you swim?","You can swim.","Swim to the side."],"answer":0,"hint":"A question asks. Where is the verb can?","explain":"Can you swim? puts the verb can first and ends with a question mark. You can swim. is a statement and Swim to the side. is a command."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A page has {n} sentences. {e} are exclamations and the rest are commands. How many commands are there?","answer":"n-e","hint":"Take the exclamations away from all the sentences.","explain":"{n} take away {e} is {=n-e}, so there are {=n-e} commands on the page.","params":{"n":[8,12],"e":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Sentences have jobs. A statement tells. My bike is red. A question asks. Where is my hat? It ends with a question mark.","An exclamation shows a big feeling. It starts with What or How. What a big dog that is! How tall you are! It ends with !","A command tells you what to do. It has a bossy verb. Shut the door. Stir the soup. The bossy verb is the doing word.","Look at the job, not just the mark. Sit down! ends with !, but it is bossy. So it is a command. To ask, move the verb to the front. The dog is hungry. Is the dog hungry?"],"adult":["The English national curriculum for Year 2 asks children to learn how the grammar of a sentence shows its function as a statement, question, exclamation or command. US Common Core L.1.1j asks first graders to produce declarative, interrogative, imperative and exclamatory sentences. These are the same four types with formal names.","Each type has a form as well as a job. A question either starts with a question word or puts the verb before the subject: you can swim becomes can you swim. A command uses the imperative form of the verb with no subject, as in wash your hands. In the curriculum, an exclamation starts with What or How and contains a verb: what a big dog that is.","Common mistakes: deciding the type from the end mark alone, so Sit down! is called an exclamation; thinking the first word of a command is always the bossy verb, when Please or Carefully can come first; calling How tall you are! a question because it starts with How; and ending a question with a full stop. Be careful that one-word shouts such as Wow! are not full exclamation sentences.","In the lab your child sorts sentences by their job, taps the bossy verb in commands, moves the verb to the front to turn a statement into a question, picks What or How to start an exclamation, and takes away you to turn a statement into a command."]},"familyTip":"Play sentence jobs at dinner. Say a sentence such as 'Pass the peas.' or 'What a big plate that is!' and let your child shout its job: tells, asks, big feeling or bossy. Then swap roles.","keyIdea":"Every sentence has a job, and its job gives its type. A statement tells you something and ends with a full stop. A question asks something and ends with a question mark. An exclamation shows a big feeling; it starts with What or How, has a verb, and ends with an exclamation mark. A command tells someone to do something; it has a bossy verb (an imperative verb) and ends with a full stop or an exclamation mark. The end mark alone does not tell you the type: Sit down! is a command.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What is each sentence's job? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Statement","Question","Exclamation","Command"],"items":[["The sun is hot.",0],["Is the sun hot?",1],["What a hot day it is!",2],["Put on your hat.",3],["Run inside now!",3],["How hot it is!",2],["Where is the shade?",1],["We sat in the shade.",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagkinds","caption":"Flagship lab: sort sentences by job, find bossy verbs, turn statements into questions, start exclamations with What or How, and make commands.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_uvILgZq9HN":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which of these is an adverbial of number?","choices":["In addition","Meanwhile","Beyond the wall"],"answer":0,"hint":"An adverbial of number orders or adds points.","explain":"In addition adds another point, so it is an adverbial of number. Meanwhile is time and Beyond the wall is place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"'Beyond the forest, the land was flat and dry.' What does the opener tell the reader?","choices":["Where the paragraph happens","When it happens","Which point comes next"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does Beyond the forest name a time, a place or a point?","explain":"Beyond the forest is an adverbial of place. It moves the reader to a new setting before the paragraph begins."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which paragraph opener is punctuated correctly?","choices":["Several hours later, the storm passed.","Several hours later the storm, passed.","Several, hours later the storm passed."],"answer":0,"hint":"The comma goes after the whole fronted adverbial.","explain":"Several hours later is one fronted adverbial, so the comma comes straight after it, before the main clause."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"The last paragraph ended on day {d} of the trip. The next one opens with 'Two days later,'. Which day of the trip is it now?","answer":"d+2","hint":"Count on the number of days the adverbial names.","explain":"Day {d} plus two days is day {=d+2}. The adverbial of time moves the story clock on.","params":{"d":[1,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"In a report, which order of paragraph openers is right?","choices":["Firstly, Secondly, Finally","Finally, Firstly, Secondly","Secondly, Firstly, Finally"],"answer":0,"hint":"Number adverbials go in the order their words say.","explain":"Firstly opens the first point, Secondly the next, and Finally the last one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A paragraph opens 'The next morning,' but its event happens later that same night. What is the slip?","choices":["The adverbial does not match the time","The comma is missing","Nothing is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the adverbial say the same thing as the paragraph?","explain":"The next morning moves the clock to a new day, but the event is the same night. Later that night would match."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why not start every paragraph with 'Then'?","choices":["It tells the reader nothing new about when or where","It is not a real word","Paragraphs must start with a noun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Then is a real word. What does it fail to tell the reader?","explain":"Then is not wrong, but repeated it gives no time, place or order. A precise adverbial guides the reader better."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A report has {n} reasons, one per paragraph. The first opens 'Firstly,' and the last opens 'Finally,'. How many paragraphs sit between them?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take away the first and the last paragraph.","explain":"{n} take away 2 is {=n-2} paragraphs in between, opened with words such as Secondly or In addition.","params":{"n":[3,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Linking paragraphs with adverbials: which opener tells the reader the next paragraph happens at the same time, somewhere else?","choices":["Meanwhile","Later that night","Finally"],"answer":0,"hint":"One of these keeps the story clock still and only changes the scene.","explain":"Meanwhile means at the same time. Later that night moves the clock forward, and Finally orders the last point."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A paragraph ends at {h}:00. The next paragraph opens with 'Three hours later,'. What hour is it now on the 24-hour clock?","answer":"h+3","hint":"The adverbial moves the story clock forward. Add the hours it names.","explain":"{h} + 3 = {=h+3}, so the new paragraph happens at {=h+3}:00. The time adverbial tells the reader that before anything else.","params":{"h":[8,18]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which adverbial of place could open a paragraph that moves the story to a beach?","choices":["Down by the shore","The next day","Secondly"],"answer":0,"hint":"An adverbial of place says where. Which one names a place?","explain":"Down by the shore moves the reader to the beach. The next day is time, and Secondly orders points in a report."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A story has {n} paragraphs. Every paragraph after the first opens with a linking adverbial. How many linking adverbials join the paragraphs?","answer":"n-1","hint":"The first paragraph has nothing before it to link to.","explain":"Each of the other {=n-1} paragraphs links back to the one before, so there are {=n-1} linking adverbials.","params":{"n":[3,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Long writing is split into paragraphs. When a new paragraph starts, the reader needs to know what has changed. A linking adverbial at the start does that job. It tells the reader when, where, or which point comes next.","Adverbials of time move the story clock: Later that night, The next morning, Earlier that day. Meanwhile is special. It means at the same time, so you can jump to a new character without moving the clock.","Adverbials of place move the camera: Beyond the river, Back at the castle, Deep in the forest. Adverbials of number order the points in a report: Firstly, Secondly, In addition, Finally.","Pick the adverbial that matches what really changes. If owls are hooting, it is night, not the next morning. Try not to start each paragraph with Then. It is not wrong, but it tells the reader nothing new. Put a comma after an adverbial at the front."],"adult":["This is Year 5 grammar in the English national curriculum, English Appendix 2: linking ideas across paragraphs using adverbials of time (later), place (nearby) and number (secondly). It serves the upper key stage 2 aim to use a wide range of devices to build cohesion within and across paragraphs. In the Common Core, W.4.3c and W.5.3c ask for transitional words and phrases to manage the sequence of events.","An adverbial is a word, phrase or clause that works like an adverb. It tells when, where or how. At the start of a paragraph it becomes a cohesive device that ties what comes next to what came before. Time adverbials shift the timeline. Meanwhile keeps the time but switches scene. Place adverbials move the setting. Number adverbials order the points in non-fiction.","Common mistakes: reading Meanwhile as later; choosing an adverbial that does not fit the content, such as The next morning for an event that same night; opening every paragraph with Then, Next or After that; using story time links in a report, or Firstly in a story; and leaving out the comma after a fronted adverbial, or putting it inside the phrase.","In the lab, learners sort adverbials by job, drop time adverbials onto a story timeline and place adverbials onto a map, order a report from its openers, and swap a run of Then openers using each paragraph's clue."]},"familyTip":"Read a chapter book together and hunt for the first words of each paragraph. Sort what you find into when, where and which point, then retell the story using three of them, such as Meanwhile, Back at home and The next morning.","keyIdea":"A linking adverbial at the start of a paragraph tells the reader what has changed since the last one. Adverbials of time move the story clock (Later that night, The next morning; Meanwhile keeps the same time but switches scene). Adverbials of place move the setting (Beyond the river, Back at the castle). Adverbials of number order the points in a report (Firstly, Secondly, In addition, Finally). Choose the one that matches the content, and put a comma after it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What job does each paragraph opener do? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Time","Place","Number"],"items":[["Several hours later",0],["Back at the castle",1],["Secondly",2],["Meanwhile",0],["Deep in the forest",1],["In addition",2],["The following week",0],["Far across the sea",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagadvl","caption":"Flagship lab: sort adverbials by job, move a reader along a story timeline and across a map, order a report from its openers, and replace a run of Then openers.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ntqNLHsj5n":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many things does this list name? We ate fish and chips, peas and ice cream.","choices":["3","4","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fish and chips is one meal. Count what the commas and the final \"and\" separate.","explain":"Fish and chips, peas, and ice cream: three things. The comma shows that fish and chips belongs together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which sentence needs no comma at all?","choices":["I like cats and dogs.","I like cats dogs and rabbits.","I like cats rabbits mice and dogs."],"answer":0,"hint":"Commas start when a list has three or more items.","explain":"Cats and dogs is a list of two, so \"and\" is enough. The other two lists need commas."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where is the slip? Sam, Priya, Leo and, Max ran.","choices":["The comma after \"and\"","The comma after \"Sam\"","The comma after \"Priya\""],"answer":0,"hint":"\"And\" already joins the last two names. Does it need a comma after it?","explain":"No comma goes straight after \"and\". Sam, Priya, Leo and Max ran."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Where is the slip? Red, blue and green, are my best colours.","choices":["The comma after \"green\"","The comma after \"Red\"","There is no slip"],"answer":0,"hint":"Green is the last item. Is the list still going?","explain":"No comma after the last item. The list is over, and the verb \"are\" follows straight on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why do we put commas in a list?","choices":["To show where each item ends","To show which word is most important","To fill the space between words"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the reader in the lab. What changed in the panel when you added a comma?","explain":"List commas separate the items, so the reader can tell where one thing ends and the next begins."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is correct?","choices":["Do you want tea, coffee or juice?","Do you want, tea coffee or juice?","Do you want tea coffee, or, juice?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lists can end with \"or\" as well as \"and\". The commas work the same way.","explain":"Tea, coffee or juice: a comma after tea, then \"or\" before the last choice."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Without the serial comma, how many commas does a list of {n} names need?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Count a comma after each name, but not after the last two, which \"and\" joins.","explain":"A list of {n} names needs {=n-2} commas when there is no comma before \"and\".","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Ella wrote: I bought, bread, jam and butter. What should she change?","choices":["Take out the comma after \"bought\"","Add a comma after \"jam\" and take out every other comma","Take out the comma after \"bread\""],"answer":0,"hint":"The list starts with the first item. Is \"bought\" part of the list?","explain":"No comma before the first item: I bought bread, jam and butter. The comma after bread is needed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which sentence uses the serial (Oxford) comma?","choices":["I saw lions, tigers, and bears.","I saw lions, tigers and bears.","I saw lions tigers and bears."],"answer":0,"hint":"The serial comma is the one just before the final \"and\".","explain":"Lions, tigers, and bears has a comma before \"and\": the serial comma. Lions, tigers and bears is also correct without it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A list of {n} things has no serial comma. You add one more thing to the list. How many commas does the new list need?","answer":"n-1","hint":"The new list has {n} + 1 items. Without the serial comma, it needs two fewer commas than items.","explain":"The new list has {=n+1} items, so it needs {=n+1} − 2 = {=n-1} commas.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Commas in lists: which sentence is punctuated correctly?","choices":["We need eggs, flour and milk.","We need, eggs flour and milk.","We need eggs flour, and, milk."],"answer":0,"hint":"Put a comma after each item except the last two, which \"and\" joins. No comma before the list starts.","explain":"We need eggs, flour and milk. One comma separates eggs from flour, and \"and\" joins the last two items."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A list has {n} items, written without the serial comma (no comma before \"and\"). How many list commas does it need?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Every item gets a comma after it, except the last one and the one just before \"and\".","explain":"{n} items need {=n-2} commas: one after each item apart from the last two, which \"and\" joins.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which list keeps every item whole?","choices":["I packed a sun hat, a towel and sun cream.","I packed a sun, hat, a towel and sun, cream.","I packed a, sun hat a towel, and sun cream."],"answer":0,"hint":"\"A sun hat\" and \"sun cream\" are each one thing. Commas go after whole items.","explain":"A comma never splits an item. \"A sun hat\", \"a towel\" and \"sun cream\" are three things, so the list needs one comma and one \"and\"."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You use the serial (Oxford) comma, so there is also a comma before \"and\". How many commas does a list of {n} items need?","answer":"n-1","hint":"With the serial comma, every item except the last one has a comma after it.","explain":"{n} items with the serial comma need {=n-1} commas: one after every item except the last.","params":{"n":[3,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A list in a sentence names three or more things. Read this: I bought apples pears plums and grapes. It runs together. Where does one thing end and the next begin?","Commas fix it. Put a comma after each item, then use \"and\" before the last one. I bought apples, pears, plums and grapes. Now the reader sees four things.","Some items take two or more words, like chocolate cake or a red pen. The comma goes after the whole item, never in the middle. We packed sandwiches, chocolate cake and orange juice. That is three things, not four.","Two things do not need a comma. Write cats and dogs, not cats, and dogs. Also, never put a comma before the first item, straight after \"and\", or after the last item."],"adult":["This is a punctuation objective in the English national curriculum. Year 2 pupils learn to use commas to separate items in a list (English Appendix 2). They apply it in later years when writing and editing. In the US, Common Core L.1.2c asks first graders to use commas to separate single words in a series. L.5.2a extends this to punctuation that separates items in a series.","Key terms: a list (or series) is three or more items, joined by the conjunction and or or before the last one. Each item can be a single word or a noun phrase, such as a big red bus. The list comma separates the items. A comma before the final and is the serial or Oxford comma. Many UK schools leave it out, and many US style guides use it. Both are correct; teach children to be consistent.","Common mistakes: a comma after every word, which splits noun phrases like chocolate cake. A comma before the list starts (I bought, apples). A comma straight after and. A comma after the last item, before the verb (Red, blue and green, are colours). A comma in a list of only two things. Another slip is using commas but leaving out the final and, which makes the list seem unfinished.","In the lab, children tap gaps to add commas and watch a panel show how many things a reader would see. They keep two-word items whole and take the comma out of a list of two. They pack a lunch box sentence and choose comma or and for each join. Finally, they fix three lists with one slip each."]},"keyIdea":"When a sentence lists three or more things, put a comma after each item to separate it from the next, and use \"and\" (or \"or\") before the last item: I bought apples, pears, plums and grapes. A comma goes after a whole item, so \"chocolate cake\" stays one thing. A list of two things just needs \"and\", with no comma.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the list punctuated correctly? Drag each sentence, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✓ Commas are right","✗ Comma slip"],"items":[["I need pens, pencils and paper.",0],["I need, pens, pencils and paper.",1],["We saw a fox, a deer and an owl.",0],["We saw a fox, and a deer.",1],["Tea, coffee, juice and milk are drinks.",0],["Tea, coffee, and, milk are drinks.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcomma","caption":"Flagship lab: tap gaps to add list commas and see how many things the reader sees, pack a lunch box list, and fix three broken lists.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_cU3LcEVkBQ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is the noun in \"the old green bus\"?","choices":["bus","green","old"],"answer":0,"hint":"The noun is the thing. Old and green describe it.","explain":"Bus is the noun, the naming word. Old and green are describing words (adjectives) that tell us about the bus."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which tells you most about the box?","choices":["the heavy, wooden box","the nice box","the box"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one helps you picture the box?","explain":"Heavy and wooden tell you what the box is like. Nice does not say what it looks like, and \"the box\" has no describing words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is in the right order?","choices":["the fluffy white rabbit","the rabbit fluffy white","fluffy the white rabbit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with the. Put the noun last.","explain":"The order is: the, then describing words, then the noun. So: the fluffy white rabbit."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which describing word fits a mouse best?","choices":["tiny","giant","good"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what a mouse looks like.","explain":"Mice are small, so tiny fits. Giant is wrong, and good does not tell us what it looks like."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Sam wrote {a} noun phrases. Each one has two describing words and one comma, like \"the cold, wet grass\". How many commas did Sam use?","answer":"a","hint":"Each noun phrase has one comma.","explain":"One comma in each of {a} noun phrases is {a} commas.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which one is the noun phrase in \"The tiny kitten hid\"?","choices":["The tiny kitten","kitten hid","tiny hid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the noun and all of its describing words. Leave out the doing word.","explain":"\"The tiny kitten\" is the noun phrase. Hid is a doing word (a verb), so it is not part of it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A line has {n} cats. You say \"the black cat\" and {k} cats still fit. How many cats did the word black rule out?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Start with all {n} cats. Take away the {k} that are black.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so black ruled out {=n-k} cats. That is how a describing word picks out which one.","params":{"n":[4,7],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is the best way to make \"the dog\" bigger?","choices":["the scruffy brown dog","the nice good lovely dog","the dog runs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Choose words that help you see the dog. A doing word does not count.","explain":"Scruffy and brown help you picture the dog. Nice, good and lovely do not say what it looks like."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is an expanded noun phrase?","choices":["the shiny red bike","the bike goes","bike red the"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the or a, then describing words, then the noun.","explain":"\"The shiny red bike\" has describing words before the noun bike. \"The bike goes\" has a doing word, so it is a sentence."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You write \"the\", then {a} describing words, then the noun \"dog\". How many words are in your noun phrase?","answer":"a+2","hint":"Count \"the\", the {a} describing words, and \"dog\".","explain":"1 word (the) + {a} describing words + 1 noun (dog) = {=a+2} words in the noun phrase.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Where does the comma go?","choices":["a tiny, shivering kitten","a, tiny shivering kitten","a tiny shivering, kitten"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could you say \"tiny and shivering\"? Put the comma where the and would go.","explain":"The comma goes between the two describing words: a tiny, shivering kitten. There is no comma after a or before the noun."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"There are {n} balls. {k} of them are red. You say \"the red ball\". How many balls could you mean?","answer":"k","where":"k<n","hint":"Which balls fit the word red?","explain":"Only the red balls fit, so you could mean {k} balls. Add another describing word to pick out just one.","params":{"n":[3,6],"k":[2,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A noun is a naming word, like cat, hat or door. \"The cat\" is a noun phrase. It is a small one.","We can make it bigger. Add describing words before the noun: the small ginger cat. Now you can see it in your head. And if there are four cats, you know which one I mean.","The order matters. Start with the or a. Then put the describing words. The noun goes last. We say \"the fluffy cat\", not \"the cat fluffy\".","Two describing words? You can put a comma between them: the old, creaky door. Pick words that tell us a lot. \"Nice\" does not tell us much. \"Enormous\" does."],"adult":["This is a Year 2 objective in the English National Curriculum (2014), Appendix 2: 'expanded noun phrases for description and specification', with examples such as 'the blue butterfly' and 'the man in the moon'. Year 2 terms for pupils are noun, noun phrase and adjective. The word determiner (the, a, my, those) comes in Year 4. The US Common Core asks for the same skill in L.1.1f and L.2.1f: use frequently occurring adjectives and produce expanded sentences.","An expanded noun phrase has a head noun plus modifiers. At this age they are mostly adjectives before the noun, sometimes a phrase after it ('the man in the moon'). The modifiers do two jobs. Description helps the reader picture the thing. Specification picks out which one: 'the small ginger cat' rules out the other cats. Commas separate two adjectives that each describe the noun, as in 'the old, creaky door'. A quick test: if you could put 'and' between them, use a comma.","Common mistakes: putting the adjective after the noun ('the cat fluffy'), adding a verb and calling it a noun phrase ('the rabbit hops' is a clause), a comma after 'the' or just before the noun, and piling up vague words ('a nice, good, lovely dog'). Be careful with stacks of colour and size words such as 'big black dog': English puts size before colour and needs no comma there. Two or three precise words beat five vague ones.","In the lab your child drags describing words onto 'the cat' and watches a line-up of four cats shrink until only the starred one fits. Then they build phrases in order, place the comma, swap a vague word for a precise one, and tap every word of the noun phrase in a sentence."]},"familyTip":"Play \"Which one?\": put four similar things on the table (cups, socks, toy cars). Take turns describing one in a noun phrase, like \"the small striped sock\", until the other person can point to it.","keyIdea":"A noun phrase is a noun with the words that go with it. To expand it, add describing words (adjectives) before the noun: \"the cat\" becomes \"the small ginger cat\". The order is: the or a, then the describing words, then the noun. Describing words do two jobs: they help the reader picture it, and they pick out which one you mean. If two describing words could have \"and\" between them, put a comma there: \"the old, creaky door\".","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it an expanded noun phrase? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Expanded noun phrase","Not one"],"items":[["the blue butterfly",0],["the dog barks",1],["a tiny, shivering kitten",0],["cat fluffy the",1],["the old, creaky door",0],["runs fast",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnph","caption":"Flagship lab: add describing words until only one cat fits, build phrases in order, place the comma, pick precise words and find the noun phrase.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_enj1sMcfOT":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is the past tense of bake?","choices":["baked","bakeed","bakt"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bake already ends in e. Do you need another e?","explain":"Bake ends in e, so just add d: baked. The ending sounds like t but is spelled ed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is the past tense of go?","choices":["went","goed","goes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Go is an odd verb. It does not take -ed.","explain":"Go changes to went for the past: today I go, yesterday I went. Goes is present."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"He ___ shouting when the bell rang. Which helper verb fits?","choices":["was","is","were"],"answer":0,"hint":"The bell rang in the past. He is just one person.","explain":"It was going on in the past and he is one person, so use was: he was shouting."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which sentence is going on right now?","choices":["The birds are singing.","The birds were singing.","The birds sang."],"answer":0,"hint":"Now needs am, is or are with an -ing verb.","explain":"Are singing is the present progressive: it is going on now. Were singing and sang are past."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Find the slip: We went to the beach. We swim in the sea. Then we ate ice cream.","choices":["swim should be swam","went should be go","ate should be eat"],"answer":0,"hint":"The story is in the past. Which verb is not?","explain":"Went and ate are past, so the story is in the past. Swim slipped into the present; it should be swam."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A list has {a} verbs that take -ed, like jump, and {b} odd verbs, like run. How many verbs in the list do not take -ed?","answer":"b","hint":"Odd verbs change their form instead of taking -ed.","explain":"Only the {b} odd verbs do not take -ed. The other {a} verbs add -ed for the past.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Tom wrote {n} sentences about yesterday. {k} of them use was + -ing, like I was reading. How many of his sentences do not use was + -ing?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Take the was + -ing sentences away from all of his sentences.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so {=n-k} sentences do not use was + -ing.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is the past tense of hop?","choices":["hopped","hoped","hopt"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hop has a short o sound and ends in one consonant.","explain":"After a short vowel and one consonant, double it: hop, hopped. Hoped is the past of hope."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which sentence tells about something that is done, in the past?","choices":["The cat jumped.","The cat is jumping.","The cat jumps."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the verb that ends in -ed. That shows the past.","explain":"Jumped ends in -ed, so it is the past tense: the jump is done. Is jumping is going on now."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A story has {a} past verbs that end in -ed, like jumped, and {b} odd past verbs, like ran. How many past tense verbs are in the story?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Odd verbs like ran are past tense too, even without -ed. Count both kinds.","explain":"{a} -ed verbs and {b} odd past verbs make {=a+b} past tense verbs.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The dogs ___ barking right now. Which helper verb fits?","choices":["are","is","were"],"answer":0,"hint":"Right now means present. The dogs are more than one.","explain":"It is going on now and there is more than one dog, so use are: the dogs are barking."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A story in the past tense has {n} verbs. {k} of them slipped into the present, like play instead of played. How many verbs are in the past tense?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Take the verbs that slipped away from all the verbs.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so {=n-k} verbs are in the past tense. The other {k} need fixing.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A verb is a doing word. The tense of a verb tells when. Past tense tells what is done. Present tense tells about now.","Lots of verbs add ed to show the past. Today I jump. Yesterday I jumped. The end can sound like t, but we still write ed.","Some verbs are odd ones. They do not add ed. Run turns into ran. Go turns into went. See turns into saw.","To show it is still going on, add ing and a helper word. Sam is running. That is now. Sam was running. That was in the past. Use is or was for one. Use are or were for more than one. When you write a story, pick past or present. Then keep all the verbs the same."],"adult":["This is the Year 2 grammar objective in the English National Curriculum (2014): correct choice and consistent use of present tense and past tense throughout writing, and the progressive form of verbs in the present and past tense to mark actions in progress (for example, she is drumming, he was shouting). Year 2 children are expected to know the terms verb and tense (past, present). In the US, Common Core L.1.1e asks children to use verbs for past, present and future, L.2.1d adds frequently used irregular past forms, and L.4.1b names the progressive tenses.","English marks two tenses on the verb itself: present (walk, walks) and past (walked). The progressive is built from a form of be plus the -ing participle: am, is or are + -ing for the present progressive, was or were + -ing for the past progressive. The helper must agree with the subject (Sam is, the birds are). The simple present (I walk to school) is mostly used for habits and facts; for an action happening at this moment English normally uses the present progressive (I am walking). Will walk is often taught as the future, but the National Curriculum glossary treats it as a modal verb with no future tense, so this lesson keeps to past and present.","The regular past ending -ed is said three ways, as in jumped (t), played (d) and wanted (id), but it is always spelled -ed. The Year 1 and Year 2 spelling rules add two changes: if the root ends in e, add only d (bake, baked), and after a short vowel and one consonant, double the consonant (hop, hopped). Hoped is a different word, the past of hope. Irregular verbs change their form instead: ran, went, saw, sat, ate.","Common mistakes: adding -ed to irregular verbs (runned, goed), which is a normal sign that the child has learned the rule and is now applying it too widely; spelling the ending by sound (jumpt); a helper that does not agree (the boys was playing); and tense drift, where a story told in the past slips into the present halfway (We went to the park. We play on the swings.). In the lab your child drags sentences onto a timeline from Yesterday to Now, builds -ed past forms, chooses is, are, was or were for an -ing verb, meets the odd verbs that do not take -ed, and taps the verb that slips out of tense in three short stories."]},"familyTip":"At tea time ask, 'What did you do today?' and listen for past verbs: played, ran, ate. Then play 'What is everyone doing now?': Dad is cooking, the cat is sleeping. If you hear runned or goed, just say the right word back: 'You ran? Wow!'","keyIdea":"The tense of a verb shows when something happens. The past tense tells what is done: most verbs add -ed (jump → jumped), and some change instead (run → ran, go → went). The present tense tells about now. To show an action going on, use a helper verb and an -ing word: is or are + -ing means it is going on now (she is drumming); was or were + -ing means it was going on in the past (he was shouting). In a piece of writing, pick a tense and keep to it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Done, or going on now? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Past: it is done","Present: going on now"],"items":[["She kicked the ball.",0],["She is kicking the ball.",1],["We ran home.",0],["We are running home.",1],["The baby cried.",0],["The baby is crying.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtense","caption":"Flagship lab: drop sentences on a timeline, spell -ed past words, pick is, are, was or were, find odd past verbs and fix the verb that slips.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_J5cx6S_eT9":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is a pronoun in 'She gave the book to Tom'?","choices":["She","the","book"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pronoun stands in for a noun, like a name.","explain":"She stands in for a girl's name, so it is a pronoun. The is a determiner and book is a noun."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which sentence is right?","choices":["Is this pen yours?","Is this pen your's?","Is this pen your?"],"answer":0,"hint":"The owner word stands alone at the end. Does it need an apostrophe?","explain":"Yours stands alone, so it is a possessive pronoun, and possessive pronouns never take an apostrophe."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In 'Some cakes are pink', what is Some?","choices":["a determiner","a pronoun","an adverbial"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a noun right after Some?","explain":"Some comes right before the noun cakes and points at it, so here it is a determiner."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In 'I ate some', what is some?","choices":["a pronoun","a determiner","a noun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at what comes after some. Is there a noun?","explain":"Nothing comes after some. It stands in for the food, so here it is a pronoun."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which phrase uses a or an correctly?","choices":["an orange","a egg","an pear"],"answer":0,"hint":"An goes before a vowel sound. A goes before a consonant sound.","explain":"Orange starts with a vowel sound, so an orange is right. It should be an egg and a pear."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which part of 'At the park, we played tag' is an adverbial?","choices":["At the park","we","tag"],"answer":0,"hint":"An adverbial tells when, where or how.","explain":"At the park tells where we played, so it is an adverbial. It is fronted, so a comma follows it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word is a possessive pronoun?","choices":["theirs","their","them"],"answer":0,"hint":"A possessive pronoun shows who owns something and stands alone.","explain":"Theirs stands alone and shows ownership: the house is theirs. Their needs a noun after it, and them is not possessive."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"In a list of words like that and each, {a} have a noun right after them and {b} stand alone. How many are determiners?","answer":"a","hint":"A determiner always has a noun after it.","explain":"The {a} words with a noun after them are determiners. The other {b} stand alone, so they are pronouns.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A page has {d} determiners. {k} of them are possessive, like my or their. How many are not possessive?","answer":"d-k","where":"k<d","hint":"Take the possessive determiners away from all the determiners.","explain":"{d} take away {k} is {=d-k}, so {=d-k} determiners are not possessive, like the, a or some.","params":{"d":[4,9],"k":[1,3]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word is a determiner in 'Two owls sat on the branch'?","choices":["Two","owls","sat"],"answer":0,"hint":"A determiner comes before a noun and points at it.","explain":"Two comes before the noun owls and tells how many, so it is a determiner. The is the other one."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In a list of words like this and some, {a} have a noun right after them and {b} stand alone. How many are pronouns?","answer":"b","hint":"A word that stands alone, with no noun after it, is doing the job of a pronoun.","explain":"The {b} words that stand alone are pronouns. The {a} words with a noun after them are determiners.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which word takes a, not an?","choices":["unicorn","umbrella","egg"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word. Does it start with a vowel sound or a consonant sound?","explain":"Unicorn starts with a y sound (you-nicorn), so it takes a: a unicorn. An umbrella and an egg start with vowel sounds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ella wrote yours {a} times and hers {b} times. How many apostrophes do those words need?","answer":"a-a","hint":"Yours and hers are possessive pronouns. Do they ever take an apostrophe?","explain":"Possessive pronouns never take an apostrophe, so all {=a+b} words need 0 apostrophes.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A determiner comes before a noun. It points at the noun. In the dog, the is a determiner. So are a, an, my, your, this, those, some, every and two.","Use an when the next word starts with a vowel sound: an apple, an hour. Use a for a consonant sound: a dog, a unicorn. Listen to the sound, not the letter.","A pronoun stands in for a noun. She, it, we and them are pronouns. Some words can do both jobs. In this cake, this points at cake. So it is a determiner. In I want this, no noun comes next. So this is a pronoun.","A possessive pronoun shows who owns a thing. It stands alone: mine, yours, his, hers, ours, theirs. It is my bag. The bag is mine. These words never have an apostrophe. An adverbial tells when, where or how: after lunch, on the peg, quickly."],"adult":["These are Year 4 terms in the English National Curriculum (2014), English Appendix 2: determiner, pronoun, possessive pronoun and adverbial. They build on the Year 3 rule for a or an. They sit beside the Year 4 work on choosing a pronoun or a noun for clarity, and on fronted adverbials. In the US, Common Core L.1.1d covers personal, possessive and indefinite pronouns, and L.1.1h covers determiners such as articles and demonstratives.","The National Curriculum glossary says a determiner marks a noun as known or unknown and goes before any adjectives. The types are articles (the, a, an), demonstratives (this, those), possessives (my, your) and quantifiers (some, every), plus numbers. Pronouns are used like nouns. Many words do both jobs. This, that, these, those, some and each are determiners before a noun and pronouns when they stand alone, so the test is whether a noun follows. My, your, her, our and their are determiners; some books call them possessive determiners, and older ones possessive adjectives. Mine, yours, hers, ours and theirs are possessive pronouns. His can be either.","A or an depends on the first sound of the next word, not its first letter. We say an hour and an honest man (the h is silent), but a unicorn and a one-off (they start with y and w sounds). An adverbial is a word, phrase or clause that works like an adverb. It says when, where, how or how often: quickly, on the peg, after lunch, when the bell rang. It can move to the front of the sentence, where it takes a comma.","Common mistakes: an apostrophe in a possessive pronoun (your's, her's, their's), often copied from Sam's; calling every this or some a determiner without checking for a noun; choosing a or an by the letter (a hour, an unicorn); and calling my a pronoun because it refers to a person. In the lab your child tags determiners and sees each one point at its noun. Then they pick a or an by sound, sort this, some and those by whether a noun follows, turn my into mine, and label every part of a sentence."]},"familyTip":"Read a cereal box or a sign together and hunt for determiners: the, a, some, every, two. For each one, ask: which noun does it point at? Then try 'Whose is it?' at home: 'Is this cup yours or mine?' and check that nobody writes your's.","keyIdea":"A determiner comes before a noun and points at it: the, a, an, my, this, those, some, every, two. Use an before a vowel sound (an hour) and a before a consonant sound (a unicorn). A pronoun stands in for a noun: she, it, they. Some words do both jobs: in 'this cake' this is a determiner, but in 'I want this' it stands alone, so it is a pronoun. A possessive pronoun stands alone and shows who owns something, with no apostrophe: mine, yours, hers, ours, theirs. An adverbial tells when, where or how.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the word in capitals a determiner or a pronoun? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Determiner: a noun comes next","Pronoun: it stands alone"],"items":[["THAT dog is fast.",0],["I like THAT.",1],["EACH child sang.",0],["They got a sticker EACH.",1],["THESE socks are odd.",0],["THESE are odd.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdetpro","caption":"Flagship lab: tag determiners and see them point at nouns, pick a or an by sound, sort this and some, turn my into mine, then label a sentence.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_u7Jxjjatkh":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the subject in 'The tall girl jumps high'?","choices":["The tall girl","jumps","high"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask yourself: who is doing the jumping? That whole group of words is the subject.","explain":"The tall girl does the jumping, so she is the subject. Jumps is the verb, and high tells how."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Pick the right verb: The frogs ___ into the pond.","choices":["hop","hops","hopes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Frogs already has an s. Only one word gets the s.","explain":"The frogs is more than one, so the verb has no s: the frogs hop. Hopes is a different word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Pick the right verb: I ___ my teeth.","choices":["brush","brushes","brushs"],"answer":0,"hint":"I and you never take the s.","explain":"I brush is right. He brushes takes es after sh, but I takes the plain verb."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Pick the right word: Sam and Leo ___ at the park.","choices":["are","is","am"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two names joined by and make more than one.","explain":"Sam and Leo are two people, so use are. Is is for one, and am only goes with I."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which sentence is wrong?","choices":["The children plays tag.","The children play tag.","The child plays tag."],"answer":0,"hint":"Children means more than one child, even without an s.","explain":"The children plays is wrong: children is plural, so the verb is play. One child plays."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Pick the right verb: She ___ the window.","choices":["fixes","fixs","fix"],"answer":0,"hint":"She is one person. After x, the verb ending is es.","explain":"She fixes is right. After s, sh, ch or x, add es, so fixs is not a word, and fix needs a plural subject."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"The {n} cats nap on the bed. Then all but one walk away. How many cats are left? (Then 'The cats nap' turns into 'The cat naps'.)","answer":"1","hint":"Take away all the cats but one. One cat left means the verb changes.","explain":"All but one walk away, so 1 cat is left. Now the cat naps, with an s on the verb.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which sentence about a pet is correct?","choices":["My dog has a ball.","My dog have a ball.","My dogs has a ball."],"answer":0,"hint":"Have works like other verbs: she has, they have.","explain":"My dog has a ball is right: one dog takes has. My dogs would need have."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why is 'The dogs barks' wrong?","choices":["Both words have an s; only one should","Dogs cannot bark","It needs more words"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the words with an s on the end.","explain":"More than one dog means the s is on dogs, so the verb is bark: the dogs bark."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"{n} friends each write one sentence about one pet, like 'My cat sleeps.' How many verbs in all end in s?","answer":"n","hint":"Each sentence has one pet, so each verb gets an s. Count the sentences.","explain":"One pet is singular, so every verb takes s: my cat sleeps. {n} sentences give {n} verbs with s.","params":{"n":[2,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Pick the verb that agrees with the subject: The bird ___ in the tree.","choices":["sings","sing","singing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the subject one bird or more than one? One subject means the verb takes s.","explain":"The bird is one, so the verb takes s: the bird sings. Sing would need more birds, and singing needs a helper like is."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In 'The dog runs', only one word ends in s. In 'The dogs run', only one word ends in s too. You write {n} sentences like these. How many s endings do you write?","answer":"n","hint":"Each sentence has exactly one s: on the verb for one dog, or on the noun for many dogs.","explain":"Each sentence has one s, so {n} sentences have {n} s endings. The s never goes on both words.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sentence has subject-verb agreement?","choices":["They play football.","They plays football.","They is playing football."],"answer":0,"hint":"They means more than one. Does the verb need an s?","explain":"They play is right: they is plural, so no s. Plays is for one, and they goes with are, not is."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"There are {n} ducks on the pond. In 'The ducks swim', how many ducks does the subject stand for?","answer":"n","hint":"The subject is the ducks. Count the ducks on the pond.","explain":"The ducks stands for all {n} ducks. Because {n} is more than one, the verb is swim, with no s.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A sentence tells us who does something. The who is the subject. The doing word is the verb. In Mia kicks the ball, Mia is the subject and kicks is the verb.","The verb has to match the subject. One dog runs. Two dogs run. Look: only one word gets the s. With one dog, the s is on runs. With two dogs, the s is on dogs.","He, she and it are one, so the verb gets an s: she sings. They and we are more than one, so no s: they sing. I and you never get the s: I sing.","Is and are work the same way. The cat is sleepy. The cats are sleepy. Tom and Ana are friends, because that is two people."],"adult":["Subject-verb agreement means the verb's form matches the number and person of its subject. In the present tense, English marks this in only one place: a third-person singular subject (he, she, it, the dog, Mia) takes a verb with the -s or -es suffix. Every other subject (I, you, we, they, the dogs) takes the plain verb. Common Core L.1.1c asks Grade 1 children to use singular and plural nouns with matching verbs in basic sentences (He hops; We hop), and L.3.1f returns to it. In England, Key Stage 1 grammar builds the terms noun, verb, singular and plural, and Year 4 asks for Standard English verb forms such as we were, not we was.","Key words: subject (who or what does the action), verb (the action word), singular (one), plural (more than one), present tense, the suffix -s or -es, pronoun (he, she, it, they, we, I, you), and agree. Be is irregular: I am, he is, they are. Have works the same way: she has, they have. After s, sh, ch or x the verb takes -es: she washes, he fixes.","Common mistakes: putting an s on both the noun and the verb (the dogs runs), because children link s with more than one; adding s after I (I runs); using is after two names joined by and (Tom and Ana is); adding s after irregular plurals such as children or people (the children plays); and writing washs in place of washes. A careful check is to find the subject first, ask whether it is one or more, then fix the verb.","In the lab your child taps the subject in three sentences, drags a single s to either the noun or the verb as the picture changes from one frog to three, picks the verb for he, they and I, chooses is or are, and taps the one wrong verb in three sentences to fix it. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Play the s swap at dinner. Say 'One cat naps.' Then hold up three fingers and let your child say 'Three cats nap.' Ask them where the s went. Swap in he, they and I as well.","keyIdea":"The subject is who or what does the action, and the verb is the action. They must agree, or match. When the subject is one person or thing, like he, she, it or the dog, add s to the verb: the dog runs. When the subject is more than one, like they, we or the dogs, the verb has no s: the dogs run. I and you also take no s: I run. Use is with one and are with more than one.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the verb need an s? Drag each subject, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["verb + s (she runs)","no s (they run)"],"items":[["he",0],["they",1],["the dog",0],["the dogs",1],["I",1],["Mia",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsva","caption":"Flagship lab: find the subject, swap the s between noun and verb, match verbs to he, they and I, choose is or are, fix wrong verbs.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_-tcJeAhK5k":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which word is a modal verb?","choices":["should","perhaps","quickly"],"answer":0,"hint":"A modal verb helps a main verb, as in you ___ go.","explain":"Should is a modal verb: you should go. Perhaps and quickly are adverbs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the parenthesis? Our teacher, Ms Cole, loves maps.","choices":["Ms Cole","Our teacher","loves maps"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lift each part out. Which one leaves a sentence that still works?","explain":"Lift out Ms Cole and you get Our teacher loves maps, which still works. So Ms Cole is the parenthesis."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is written correctly?","choices":["You should have asked.","You should of asked.","You should asked."],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly. The word after should is have, even if it sounds like of.","explain":"Should have is right. Should of is a common slip because should've sounds like should of."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The forecast says a 10% chance of snow. Which sentence fits best?","choices":["It might snow.","It will snow.","It must snow."],"answer":0,"hint":"10% is a small chance. Which modal is weakest?","explain":"A small chance needs a weak modal, so it might snow. Will and must would sound certain."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word starts a relative clause about a place?","choices":["where","who","whose"],"answer":0,"hint":"This is the park ___ we play. Which word means at that place?","explain":"Where is for places: this is the park where we play. Who is for people and whose shows belonging."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Your paragraph uses {n} modal verbs and {k} adverbs such as perhaps and surely. How many of these words are verbs?","answer":"n","hint":"Perhaps and surely show possibility, but are they verbs?","explain":"Only the {n} modal verbs are verbs. The {k} adverbs show possibility too, but they are not verbs.","params":{"n":[2,6],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You write {n} sentences, each with a parenthesis in brackets. How many brackets do you type?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each parenthesis needs an opening bracket and a closing bracket.","explain":"Each one needs 2 brackets, so {n} sentences need {=2*n} brackets.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"I saw the man with the telescope. Why is this sentence ambiguous?","choices":["It has two possible meanings","It has no verb","It is too short"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who has the telescope? Could it be either person?","explain":"It is ambiguous: either you used the telescope, or the man had it. Rewording can make the meaning clear."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which sentence is the most certain?","choices":["We will win the match.","We might win the match.","We could win the match."],"answer":0,"hint":"Put might, could and will on a scale from possible to certain.","explain":"Will is certain. Might and could only say that winning is possible."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A sentence has {n} words. Only one of them is a modal verb. How many words are not modal verbs?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the one modal verb away from the {n} words.","explain":"{n} words take away 1 modal verb leaves {=n-1} words. A sentence usually has just one modal, right before the main verb.","params":{"n":[5,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Pick the right word: The man ___ lives next door has a parrot.","choices":["who","which","where"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the man a person, a thing or a place?","explain":"Who is for people, so it starts this relative clause. Which is for things and where is for places."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You write {n} sentences. Each one has a parenthesis inside commas, like My dog, a cheeky terrier, ate my sock. How many commas do you use?","answer":"2*n","hint":"A parenthesis in commas needs one comma before it and one after it.","explain":"Each parenthesis needs 2 commas, so {n} sentences need {=2*n} commas. A common slip is to leave off the second one.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some verbs are helpers. They sit before the main verb and tell us how sure we are. These helpers are called modal verbs. It might rain means it is possible. It should rain means it is likely. It will rain means it is certain. Must is strong too: you must wear a seatbelt.","Can, could, may, would and shall are modal verbs as well. Words like maybe and perhaps show how sure we are, but they are adverbs, not verbs.","A relative clause adds detail about a noun. The girl who won the race is my sister. Use who for people, which for things and where for places.","A parenthesis is extra information. My dog, a cheeky terrier, ate my sock. Take out a cheeky terrier and the sentence still works. Put commas, brackets or dashes round it."],"adult":["This is Year 5 grammar in the English national curriculum (Appendix 2). Children show degrees of possibility with modal verbs, such as might, should, will and must, or with adverbs, such as perhaps and surely. They write relative clauses that begin with who, which, where, when, whose or that, or with the relative pronoun left out. They use brackets, dashes or commas to mark parenthesis, and commas to make meaning clear or to avoid ambiguity. They also build cohesion in a paragraph with words like then, after that and this.","The terms children must know are modal verb, relative pronoun, relative clause, parenthesis, bracket, dash, cohesion and ambiguity. A modal verb never changes for the subject: she can, not she cans. It is followed by the plain form of the main verb: you should go. Modal verbs can also show ability (can), permission (may) or duty (must), not just how likely something is.","Common mistakes: calling maybe or perhaps a modal verb, when they are adverbs; forgetting that can and would are modals; using which for people (the man which); and writing should of for should have. With parenthesis, a frequent slip is putting a comma before the extra part but not after it. Children also think parenthesis only means brackets. Ask them to lift the part out and read what is left.","In the lab your child drags modal verbs onto a possible, likely or certain scale. They turn a dial until the modal matches a weather forecast. They tap the modal verb in a sentence that has maybe as a trap. They drop who, which or where into a relative clause, and they lift out a parenthesis to prove the sentence still works."]},"familyTip":"Watch a weather forecast together and turn it into modal sentences: at 20% it might rain, at 90% it will probably rain. Then ask your child to add a parenthesis to one sentence about the weather.","keyIdea":"Modal verbs (can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would, must) help a main verb and show how sure or how able we are: might and could are possible, should is likely, will and must are certain. Adverbs such as perhaps and surely also show possibility, but they are not verbs. A relative clause adds detail about a noun and starts with who, which, where, when, whose or that. A parenthesis is extra information inside commas, brackets or dashes; lift it out and the sentence still works.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Possible or certain? Drag each word to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Possible","Certain"],"items":[["might",0],["will",1],["could",0],["must",1],["may",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmodal","caption":"Flagship lab: place modal verbs on a certainty scale, match a dial to the forecast, spot the modal verb, fill relative clauses and lift out a parenthesis.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_gbTyzvnWzr":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In The bridge was built by Romans, who did the building?","choices":["the Romans","the bridge","We are not told"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the passive, the doer comes after the word by.","explain":"The Romans are the agent. The bridge is the subject but it is the receiver of the building."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is NOT passive?","choices":["We were tired.","We were chosen.","We were chosen by the coach."],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the word after were a past participle, or a describing word?","explain":"Tired here describes how we felt, so We were tired is not a passive. Were chosen is be + past participle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Pick the correct passive: The storm ___ the roof off.","choices":["The roof was blown off by the storm.","The roof was blew off by the storm.","The roof blown off by the storm."],"answer":0,"hint":"You need a form of be and the past participle of blow.","explain":"Blow, blew, blown: blown is the past participle, and was is the form of be."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why does a lab report write The mixture was filtered?","choices":["The process matters more than who did it","The passive is always better writing","It hides a mistake"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who would need to repeat the method, and what do they need to know?","explain":"A method needs the steps, not the name of the person, so the passive keeps the focus on the process."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which sentence is an agentless passive?","choices":["The shop was closed early.","The shop was closed by the owner.","The owner closed the shop."],"answer":0,"hint":"Agentless means no by-phrase naming the doer.","explain":"The shop was closed early is passive with no by-phrase, so the doer is not named."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which version suits an adventure story best?","choices":["The knight drew his sword and charged.","The sword was drawn by the knight.","A charge was made."],"answer":0,"hint":"Stories feel fast when the doer leads the sentence.","explain":"Active voice puts the knight first, doing things, which keeps the action lively."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A news report has {a} passive sentences. {b} of them name the doer with by. How many are agentless?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Agentless passives are the ones without a by-phrase.","explain":"{a} passives minus {b} with a by-phrase leaves {=a-b} agentless passives.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Make it passive: The judges choose the winners.","choices":["The winners are chosen by the judges.","The winners is chosen by the judges.","The winners chose the judges."],"answer":0,"hint":"Winners is plural. Which present form of be agrees with it?","explain":"Are agrees with winners, and chosen is the past participle of choose."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A report has {a} sentences. {b} of them are passive. How many are in the active voice?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Every sentence with a verb is either active or passive. Take away the passives.","explain":"{a} sentences minus {b} passives leaves {=a-b} active sentences.","params":{"a":[8,14],"b":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which sentence is in the passive voice?","choices":["The letter was posted by Kai.","Kai posted the letter.","Kai was posting the letter."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the receiver as subject, then a form of be plus a past participle.","explain":"The letter was posted by Kai is passive: the letter is the subject and was posted is be + past participle. Kai was posting is active, because Kai is still the doer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A science method has {a} passive sentences and {b} active sentences. How many sentences use be + a past participle with the receiver as subject?","answer":"a","hint":"Only passive sentences have the receiver as subject with be + past participle.","explain":"Only the {a} passive sentences do. The {b} active sentences keep the doer as subject.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Make it passive: The children painted the fence.","choices":["The fence was painted by the children.","The fence painted the children.","The fence were painted by the children."],"answer":0,"hint":"Fence is one thing, so which form of be agrees with it?","explain":"The fence becomes the subject, so it takes was, and painted stays as the past participle. Swapping the nouns changes the meaning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Zara rewrites {n} active sentences as passives and cuts the by-phrase from {k} of them. How many of her passives still name the doer?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Each passive starts with a by-phrase. Take away the ones she cut.","explain":"{n} passives minus {k} cut by-phrases leaves {=n-k} that still name the doer. The rest are agentless passives.","params":{"n":[5,10],"k":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Most sentences have a doer and a receiver. In Sam broke the window, Sam does the breaking. The window gets broken. Sam comes first, so the sentence is active.","We can flip it. The window was broken by Sam. Now the window comes first. The verb changes to was broken. That is the passive voice.","To make the passive, use a form of be, like is, are, was or were. Add the past participle, like broken, made or written. The doer can go after by, or it can go.","Use active when you want clear, lively writing. Use passive when the action matters more than who did it. It also helps when you do not know who did it: My bike was stolen."],"adult":["In England, Year 6 pupils learn to use passive verbs to change how a sentence presents information. They learn the terms active and passive. The curriculum's own example is I broke the window in the greenhouse versus The window in the greenhouse was broken (by me).","To form the passive, the object of the active sentence becomes the subject. The verb becomes a form of be plus the past participle. Be must agree with the new subject: The meals are cooked by the chef. The doer is called the agent. It moves into an optional by-phrase. A passive without one, such as The match was cancelled, is an agentless passive.","Common mistakes: using the simple past instead of the participle (was broke, was blew). Thinking any sentence with was is passive, so The cake was delicious or Tom was running get called passive. Thinking the first noun must be the doer. Believing the passive is always more formal or better. Be careful with get passives such as got stolen: they are passive too, but informal.","In the lab, learners find the doer in active and passive sentences. They build passives from chunks and avoid a wrong participle or a wrong form of be. They sort real passives from look-alikes and decide when to keep or cut the by-phrase. Last, they choose the voice that fits a science method, a story and a news report."]},"familyTip":"Read a news headline together, such as Road closed after crash. Ask: what happened, and do we know who did it? Then say it both ways, active and passive, and talk about which one the reporter needed.","keyIdea":"In the active voice the doer is the subject: Sam broke the window. In the passive voice the receiver of the action becomes the subject, and the verb is a form of be plus the past participle: The window was broken (by Sam). The doer can move into a by-phrase or be left out. Writers choose the passive to put the focus on the action or the thing it happened to, or when the doer is unknown or does not matter.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Active or passive? Drag each card to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Active","Passive"],"items":[["The fox ate the eggs.",0],["The eggs were eaten.",1],["Priya won the race.",0],["The race was won by Priya.",1],["The baby was sleeping.",0],["The cakes are baked daily.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagvoice","caption":"Flagship lab: find the doer, flip sentences into the passive, spot look-alikes, keep or cut the by-phrase, then choose a voice for a purpose.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_hzkNpp2PdV":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the object in The chef chopped the onions?","choices":["the onions","the chef","chopped"],"answer":0,"hint":"The object comes after the verb and receives the action.","explain":"The onions receive the chopping, so they are the object. The chef is the subject and chopped is the verb."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word is an antonym of generous?","choices":["mean","kind","giving"],"answer":0,"hint":"An antonym means the opposite, not the same.","explain":"Mean is the opposite of generous. Kind and giving are close in meaning, so they are synonyms."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which sentence is in the passive voice?","choices":["The match was won by our team.","Our team won the match.","Our team was happy."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for be plus a past participle with the receiver as subject.","explain":"The match was won is passive: the match is the subject and was won is be plus a past participle. Our team was happy has no action done to it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which shows a comma splice?","choices":["The sun set, the sky turned pink.","The sun set; the sky turned pink.","As the sun set, the sky turned pink."],"answer":0,"hint":"A comma splice joins two full sentences with only a comma.","explain":"The sun set and the sky turned pink are both full sentences, so a comma alone cannot join them. A semi-colon can."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why is there a hyphen in a man-eating shark?","choices":["It joins man and eating into one describing word","It shows a pause","It starts a list"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the shark does and what the man does.","explain":"The hyphen makes man-eating one word that describes the shark. Without it, it sounds like a man eating a shark."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which sentence has no object?","choices":["The guests arrived late.","The guests ate the cake.","The guests thanked the host."],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask who or what received the action. Is there anything?","explain":"Arrived takes no object, and late tells you when. Ate the cake and thanked the host both have an object."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A poster has {a} bullet points in one list and {b} in another. Each bullet point starts one item. How many items are there?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Each bullet point stands for one item, so add the two lists.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} items, one for each bullet point.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a quote, what does an ellipsis like this show: We will … win?","choices":["Some words have been left out","The writer is shouting","A list is coming"],"answer":0,"hint":"Three dots inside a quote mark a gap.","explain":"An ellipsis inside a quote shows that words were cut. It does not add emphasis or start a list."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Mia finds {s} synonyms and {t} antonyms for the word cold. How many more synonyms than antonyms does she find?","answer":"s-t","hint":"Subtract the number of antonyms from the number of synonyms.","explain":"{s} − {t} = {=s-t} more synonyms, like chilly and icy, than antonyms, like hot.","params":{"s":[5,8],"t":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In The letter was posted by Kai, what is the subject?","choices":["the letter","Kai","posted"],"answer":0,"hint":"The subject comes before the verb, even when it is not the doer.","explain":"The sentence is passive, so the letter is the subject and Kai, the doer, sits in the by-phrase. Posted is part of the verb."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A paragraph has {a} sentences joined with a semi-colon and {b} sentences that end with an ellipsis. Each semi-colon joins exactly two full sentences. How many full sentences sit either side of the semi-colons?","answer":"2*a","hint":"A semi-colon always has one full sentence before it and one after it.","explain":"Each of the {a} semi-colons links two full sentences, so 2 × {a} = {=2*a}. The {b} ellipses do not join anything.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sentence uses a colon correctly?","choices":["We packed three things: a tent, a torch and a map.","We packed: three things, a tent.","The tent was up: and we ate."],"answer":0,"hint":"A colon comes after a complete clause and points ahead to a list or explanation.","explain":"We packed three things is complete on its own, and the colon introduces the list. The other colons split a sentence in the wrong place."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Leo writes {n} sentences. {k} of them use a verb with no object, like slept. How many of his sentences have an object?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take away the sentences whose verb has no object.","explain":"{n} sentences minus the {k} with no object leaves {=n-k} sentences with an object.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Every sentence has a subject. It is who or what the sentence is about, and it usually comes before the verb. In Priya painted a tiger, Priya is the subject. The tiger is the object, because it receives the action.","Not every verb takes an object. In The baby slept all night, nothing gets slept, so there is no object. In a passive sentence, like The window was broken by the ball, the window is the subject even though the ball did the breaking.","A synonym means the same as another word, like huge and enormous. An antonym means the opposite, like ancient and modern. Knowing both helps you pick the exact word you need.","Punctuation marks have jobs. A hyphen joins two words into one, as in man-eating shark. A colon points ahead to a list or a reason. A semi-colon joins two full sentences that belong together. An ellipsis, three dots, shows a pause or words left out. Bullet points split a list into lines."],"adult":["The English national curriculum lists these terms in the Year 6 grammar appendix: subject, object, active, passive, synonym, antonym, ellipsis, hyphen, colon, semi-colon and bullet points. Pupils are expected to use the terms when they talk about their own writing, not just recognise them on a worksheet.","The subject is defined by its place and its link to the verb, not by who does the action. In a passive clause the subject is the receiver, the verb is a form of be plus a past participle, and the doer can appear in a by-phrase. Intransitive verbs such as slept, arrived or laughed take no object at all.","Common mistakes: calling the doer the subject in every sentence, so The cake was eaten by Max gets Max as subject. Treating any noun after the verb as an object, such as all night. Joining two full sentences with a comma (a comma splice). Putting a semi-colon before a list, or a colon between two equal sentences. Using an ellipsis as a full stop with extra dots.","In the lab, learners tap the subject and object of four sentences, including one with no object and one in the passive. They decide where a hyphen changes meaning, choose a colon, semi-colon or comma between clauses, and match the ellipsis, bullet points and hyphen to their jobs. The challenge adds synonyms, antonyms and active versus passive."]},"familyTip":"Pick a sentence from a reading book and take turns naming its subject and object. Then hunt for one colon, semi-colon, hyphen or ellipsis on the page and say what job it is doing there.","keyIdea":"Year 6 writers use exact terms. The subject is who or what a sentence is about; the object receives the action of the verb. In the active voice the subject does the action; in the passive voice the subject has the action done to it. Synonyms mean the same, antonyms mean the opposite. A hyphen joins words into one, a colon introduces a list or an explanation, a semi-colon joins two linked full sentences, an ellipsis shows a pause or missing words, and bullet points set out a list down the page.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Colon or semi-colon? Drag each sentence to the mark it needs, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Needs a colon","Needs a semi-colon"],"items":[["Bring three things _ a pen, a ruler and a rubber.",0],["It was late _ the shops had shut.",1],["She had one goal _ to win the cup.",0],["Tom likes maths _ Ana prefers art.",1],["You will need _ flour, eggs and butter.",0],["The bell rang _ everyone ran outside.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmarks","caption":"Flagship lab: tap subjects and objects, use a hyphen to change meaning, choose a colon or semi-colon, then match each mark to its job.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_IdFxLz-UW9":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"\"Covered in mud, Mum washed the dog.\" By the grammar, who is covered in mud?","choices":["Mum","The dog","Nobody"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first noun after the comma.","explain":"An opening phrase describes the first noun after the comma, so the sentence says Mum is muddy. That is a dangling modifier."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why is \"before dawn\" a phrase and not a clause?","choices":["It has no verb","It is only two words","It comes at the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does every clause need?","explain":"A clause has a verb. \"Before dawn\" has none, so it is a phrase, whatever its length or place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In \"We swam in the lake\", what does \"in the lake\" describe?","choices":["The verb swam: it tells where","The noun we: it tells which ones","Nothing: it is extra"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: where, when or how did something happen?","explain":"It tells where we swam, so it modifies the verb. That is an adverbial job."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which sentence has a misplaced modifier?","choices":["The man fed the dog in a tuxedo.","The man in a tuxedo fed the dog.","In a tuxedo, the man fed the dog."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which version puts the tuxedo next to the dog?","explain":"\"the dog in a tuxedo\" says the dog wears it. The other two put the phrase by the man."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What do the commas do in \"My sister, who lives in Leeds, is a nurse\"?","choices":["They add an extra fact about my sister","They pick out one sister from many","They join two main clauses"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the clause needed to know which sister?","explain":"With commas the clause is extra information about my sister. Without them, it would pick out the sister who lives in Leeds from others."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} dogs are in the park and {k} are barking. \"The dogs that are barking belong to Jo.\" By this sentence, how many dogs belong to Jo?","answer":"k","where":"k<n","hint":"A clause with no commas picks out which dogs.","explain":"\"that are barking\" picks out the {k} barking dogs, so {k} dogs belong to Jo.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is the best fix for \"After finishing my homework, the TV was switched on\"?","choices":["After finishing my homework, I switched on the TV.","After finishing my homework the TV was switched on.","The TV, after finishing my homework, was switched on."],"answer":0,"hint":"Who finished the homework?","explain":"I finished the homework, so I must come straight after the comma. Moving or dropping the comma does not fix it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A sentence has {a} adjectival phrases and {b} adverbial phrases. How many phrases describe a verb?","answer":"b","hint":"Adverbial phrases tell where, when or how about a verb.","explain":"Only the adverbial ones describe the verb, so {b} phrases do.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"\"On the table\" is adjectival in one sentence. Which?","choices":["The cup on the table is mine.","Put the cup on the table.","She sat on the table."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one uses the phrase to tell you which cup?","explain":"In \"the cup on the table is mine\" it tells which cup. In the others it tells where something happens."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In \"The boy in the photo is my cousin\", what job does \"in the photo\" do?","choices":["Adjectival: it tells which boy","Adverbial: it tells where he is going","It is a main clause"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the phrase pick out which boy, or tell you about an action?","explain":"It picks out which boy, so it does the job of an adjective. It has no verb, so it is a phrase, not a clause."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A class has {n} pupils and {k} of them had a ticket. \"The pupils who had a ticket got in.\" How many pupils got in?","answer":"k","where":"k<n","hint":"With no commas, the clause picks out which pupils.","explain":"The clause \"who had a ticket\" picks out a group, so only those {k} pupils got in.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sentence fixes \"Running for the bus, my bag fell open\"?","choices":["Running for the bus, I felt my bag fall open.","Running for the bus my bag fell open.","My bag, running for the bus, fell open."],"answer":0,"hint":"Who was really running? Put that person straight after the comma.","explain":"The opener describes the first noun after the comma, so it must be I, the person running, not the bag."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A tray holds {n} cakes. \"The cakes, which Mia baked, were sold.\" How many cakes were sold?","answer":"n","hint":"With commas, the clause just adds a fact. Does it pick out some cakes?","explain":"The commas make \"which Mia baked\" extra information about all the cakes, so all {n} were sold.","params":{"n":[4,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Where should \"with huge teeth\" go in \"I saw a shark on holiday\"?","choices":["Straight after \"shark\"","Straight after \"holiday\"","At the very start"],"answer":0,"hint":"What has the huge teeth? Put the phrase right next to it.","explain":"The shark has the teeth, so the phrase goes next to it: \"On holiday, I saw a shark with huge teeth.\""}],"explain":{"kid":["A phrase is a group of words with no verb of its own, like \"after lunch\" or \"with the red kite\". A clause has a verb, like \"who finished early\".","Lots of phrases describe something. In \"The girl with the red kite ran\", the phrase tells you which girl. That job is adjectival. In \"She ran across the field\", the phrase tells you where she ran. That job is adverbial.","A phrase describes what it sits next to. \"The man fed the dog in a tuxedo\" puts the tuxedo on the dog. Move the phrase: \"The man in a tuxedo fed the dog.\" An opening phrase describes the first noun after the comma. \"Covered in mud, Mum washed the dog\" says Mum is muddy. Write \"Covered in mud, the dog was washed by Mum.\"","Commas matter too. \"The students who finished early went outside\" means only some went. \"The students, who finished early, went outside\" means all of them went, and all of them finished early."],"adult":["Ages 11 to 13. This is Common Core L.7.1a, explain the function of phrases and clauses in general and in specific sentences, and L.7.1c, place phrases and clauses within a sentence, recognising and correcting misplaced and dangling modifiers. It matches the Key Stage 3 grammar aims in the National Curriculum in England. Key words: phrase, clause, main clause, subordinate clause, relative clause, modifier, adjectival, adverbial, misplaced, dangling.","Form and function are different questions. A prepositional phrase such as \"on the table\" is adjectival in \"the cup on the table is mine\" and adverbial in \"put the cup on the table\". Ask what it tells you: which one, or where, when or how. A relative clause without commas is defining and picks out a subset; with commas it is non-defining and adds a fact about the whole group.","Common mistakes: reading a misplaced modifier the way the writer meant rather than the way the words say; fixing a dangling opener with a comma instead of naming the doer; judging a phrase by its first word rather than its job; and adding commas round a defining relative clause, which changes who is included. Be careful: the fix for a dangler is usually to put the real doer straight after the comma.","In the lab your child links three phrases to the word each describes. They act out a relative clause with and without commas and watch who walks outside. Then they slide a phrase until it sits by its noun, choose endings that fix dangling openers and sort one phrase type by its two jobs."]},"familyTip":"Hunt for funny misplaced modifiers in signs and headlines, such as \"Dog for sale: eats anything and loves children\". Draw what the words literally say, then rewrite it. Ask: what does this phrase sit next to, and is that what it should describe?","keyIdea":"A phrase is a group of words with no verb of its own; a clause has a verb. Many phrases and clauses are modifiers: they describe a noun (adjectival, telling which one) or a verb (adverbial, telling where, when or how). A modifier describes what it sits next to, so a misplaced one changes the meaning: \"the dog in a tuxedo\". An opening phrase describes the first noun after the comma, so \"Covered in mud, Mum washed the dog\" says Mum was muddy; that is a dangling modifier. Commas round a relative clause change its job: without them it picks out which ones, with them it adds a fact about all.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the modifier next to the word it describes, or misplaced? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Clear","Misplaced or dangling"],"items":[["The man in a tuxedo fed the dog.",0],["The man fed the dog in a tuxedo.",1],["Walking home, I saw a fox.",0],["Walking home, the rain soaked me.",1],["She served cake on paper plates to the children.",0],["She served cake to the children on paper plates.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmodif","caption":"Flagship lab: link phrases to their words, act out a relative clause with and without commas, fix misplaced and dangling modifiers, and sort phrase jobs.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
